The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 CWE-787 (Out-of-bounds Write) / CVE-2019-1010258
[Mandatory] Summary:
 A vulnerability exists in the `nsvg__parseColorHex` function in `src/nanosvg.h`. The function uses `sscanf` with the `"%x"` format specifier to read a hexadecimal color string into an unsigned integer without proper bounds checking on the input string length. This can lead to an out-of-bounds write when a specially crafted long string is provided, corrupting memory.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 Unchecked buffer length passed to `sscanf`.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
SVG file parser
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Memory corruption leading to potential RCE or DoS
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
 Malicious SVG file containing a long color hex string
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

static unsigned int nsvg__parseColorHex(const char* str)
{
	unsigned int c = 0, r = 0, g = 0, b = 0;
	int n = 0;
	str++; // skip #
	// Calculate number of characters.
	while(str[n] && !nsvg__isspace(str[n]))
		n++;
	if (n == 6) {
		sscanf(str, "%x", &c);
	} else if (n == 3) {
		sscanf(str, "%x", &c);
		c = (c&0xf) | ((c&0xf0) << 4) | ((c&0xf00) << 8);
		c |= c<<4;
	}
	r = (c >> 16) & 0xff;
	g = (c >> 8) & 0xff;
	b = c & 0xff;
	return NSVG_RGB(r,g,b);
}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
SVG file parsing via nsvg__parseColorHex function

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write, Denial of Service

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is triggered by parsing a malicious SVG file, and exploitation depends on the memory layout of the application using the nanosvg library.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create an SVG file with a color attribute containing a hex string longer than 6 characters (e.g., `fill="#aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"`).
- Load the SVG file using an application that incorporates the vulnerable nanosvg library.
- The `nsvg__parseColorHex` function will be called with the malicious string.
- The unchecked `sscanf` operation will process the long string, leading to an out-of-bounds write.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static unsigned int nsvg__parseColorHex(const char* str)
{
	unsigned int r=0, g=0, b=0;
	if (sscanf(str, "#%2x%2x%2x", &r, &g, &b) == 3 )		// 2 digit hex
		return NSVG_RGB(r, g, b);
	if (sscanf(str, "#%1x%1x%1x", &r, &g, &b) == 3 )		// 1 digit hex, e.g. #abc -> 0xccbbaa
		return NSVG_RGB(r*17, g*17, b*17);			// same effect as (r<<4|r), (g<<4|g), ..
	return NSVG_RGB(128, 128, 128);
}
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
 The patch replaces the manual length calculation and unsafe `sscanf` call with two separate `sscanf` calls using strict format strings ("#%2x%2x%2x" and "#%1x%1x%1x") to parse 3-digit and 6-digit hex colors, preventing out-of-bounds writes.

[Optional] Patch reference:
 https://github.com/memononen/nanosvg/commit/419782d3d16e88cfa0695aa0b86c956352846712
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType: Logic refactor with implicit bounds checking via format string
