void JPXStream::init()
{
  Object oLen, cspace, smaskInData;
  if (getDict()) {
    oLen = getDict()->lookup("Length");
    cspace = getDict()->lookup("ColorSpace");
    smaskInData = getDict()->lookup("SMaskInData");
  }

  int bufSize = BUFFER_INITIAL_SIZE;
  if (oLen.isInt()) bufSize = oLen.getInt();

  bool indexed = false;
  if (cspace.isArray() && cspace.arrayGetLength() > 0) {
    const Object cstype = cspace.arrayGet(0);
    if (cstype.isName("Indexed")) indexed = true;
  }

  priv->smaskInData = 0;
  if (smaskInData.isInt()) priv->smaskInData = smaskInData.getInt();

  int length = 0;
  unsigned char *buf = str->toUnsignedChars(&length, bufSize);
  priv->init2(OPJ_CODEC_JP2, buf, length, indexed);
  gfree(buf);

  if (priv->image) {
    int numComps = (priv->image) ? priv->image->numcomps : 1;
    int alpha = 0;
    if (priv->image) {
      if (priv->image->color_space == OPJ_CLRSPC_SRGB && numComps == 4) { numComps = 3; alpha = 1; }
      else if (priv->image->color_space == OPJ_CLRSPC_SYCC && numComps == 4) { numComps = 3; alpha = 1; }
      else if (numComps == 2) { numComps = 1; alpha = 1; }
      else if (numComps > 4) { numComps = 4; alpha = 1; }
      else { alpha = 0; }
    }
    priv->npixels = priv->image->comps[0].w * priv->image->comps[0].h;
    priv->ncomps = priv->image->numcomps;
    if (alpha == 1 && priv->smaskInData == 0) priv->ncomps--;
    for (int component = 0; component < priv->ncomps; component++) {
      if (priv->image->comps[component].data == nullptr) {
        close();
        break;
      }
      const int componentPixels = priv->image->comps[component].w * priv->image->comps[component].h;
      if (componentPixels != priv->npixels) {
        error(errSyntaxWarning, -1, "Component {0:d} has different WxH than component 0", component);
        close();
        break;
      }
      unsigned char *cdata = (unsigned char *)priv->image->comps[component].data;
      int adjust = 0;
      int depth = priv->image->comps[component].prec;
      if (priv->image->comps[component].prec > 8)
	adjust = priv->image->comps[component].prec - 8;
      int sgndcorr = 0;
      if (priv->image->comps[component].sgnd)
	sgndcorr = 1 << (priv->image->comps[0].prec - 1);
      for (int i = 0; i < priv->npixels; i++) {
	int r = priv->image->comps[component].data[i];
	*(cdata++) = adjustComp(r, adjust, depth, sgndcorr, indexed);
      }
    }
  } else {
    priv->npixels = 0;
  }

  priv->counter = 0;
  priv->ccounter = 0;
  priv->inited = true;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2019-12293

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2019-12293 / CWE-125: NA

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2019-05-23 — Patch release: 2019-05-23

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is located in the `JPXStream::init()` function within the Poppler library when processing JPEG2000 streams. It is triggered by a crafted JPEG2000 image where different components have mismatched dimensions (width/height). The vulnerability is exploitable because the code calculates the total number of pixels (`priv->npixels`) based on the first component and then uses this value to iterate through the data of all subsequent components, leading to an out-of-bounds read on any component smaller than the first, as shown in the loop processing `priv->image->comps[component].data[i]`.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Missing bounds check on component dimensions during JPEG2000 stream processing.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
PDF renderer (poppler) processing a malicious JPEG2000 stream.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Information disclosure / Denial of Service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A crafted PDF file containing a JPEG2000 image with components of differing dimensions.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
for (int component = 0; component < priv->ncomps; component++) {
  if (priv->image->comps[component].data == nullptr) {
    close();
    break;
  }
  unsigned char *cdata = (unsigned char *)priv->image->comps[component].data;
  int adjust = 0;
  int depth = priv->image->comps[component].prec;
  if (priv->image->comps[component].prec > 8)
    adjust = priv->image->comps[component].prec - 8;
  int sgndcorr = 0;
  if (priv->image->comps[component].sgnd)
    sgndcorr = 1 << (priv->image->comps[0].prec - 1);
  for (int i = 0; i < priv->npixels; i++) {
    int r = priv->image->comps[component].data[i];
    *(cdata++) = adjustComp(r, adjust, depth, sgndcorr, indexed);
  }
}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
A crafted PDF file containing a JPEG2000 image.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read (out-of-bounds).

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is straightforward, as an attacker can control the dimensions of image components to trigger a predictable out-of-bounds read.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a JPEG2000 image with multiple components.
- Set the width and height of the first component to a large value.
- Set the width and height of a subsequent component to a smaller value.
- Embed this image in a PDF file.
- Open the PDF with a vulnerable version of Poppler to trigger the out-of-bounds read.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
const int componentPixels = priv->image->comps[component].w * priv->image->comps[component].h;
if (componentPixels != priv->npixels) {
  error(errSyntaxWarning, -1, "Component {0:d} has different WxH than component 0", component);
  close();
  break;
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a check to ensure all image components have the same dimensions (width and height) as the first component before processing them.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://cgit.freedesktop.org/poppler/poppler/commit/?id=89a5367d49b2556a2635dbb6d48d6a6b182a2c6c
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other image parsers in Poppler for similar assumptions about component dimensions.
- Fuzz JPEG2000 and other image format parsers with malformed component data.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds reads could exist in other parts of the image processing code.

