	  /* Skip the position within the line.  */
	  xptr += 2;

	  /* And finally the address.  */
	  aUnit->linenumber_table[eachLine].addr
	    = base + bfd_get_32 (stash->abfd, (bfd_byte *) xptr);
	  xptr += 4;
	}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Maliciously crafted binary file with a DWARF1 .line section

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
Exploitation is straightforward, requiring an attacker to provide a malicious binary file that triggers the out-of-bounds read, potentially leaking adjacent memory contents.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
Not available in input

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
if (xptr + 8 <= stash->line_section_end)
  {
      ...
      for (eachLine = 0; eachLine < aUnit->line_count; eachLine++)
      {
          if (xptr + 10 > stash->line_section_end)
          {
              aUnit->line_count = eachLine;
              break;
          }
          /* A line number. */
          aUnit->linenumber_table[eachLine].linenumber = bfd_get_32 (stash->abfd, (bfd_byte *) xptr);
          xptr += 4;
          /* Skip the position within the line. */
          xptr += 2;
          /* And finally the address. */
          aUnit->linenumber_table[eachLine].addr = base + bfd_get_32 (stash->abfd, (bfd_byte *) xptr);
          xptr += 4;
      }
  }

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
 The patch adds proper bounds checks to the `parse_line_table` function. It ensures that there is enough data remaining in the buffer before reading the initial header fields and before each line table entry in the loop.

[Optional] Patch reference:
