annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-13723

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-13723 / CWE-119: Buffer overflow in XKB string generation

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-10-10 — Patch release: 2017-07-27

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 CWE-119 / CVE-2017-13723 — Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
 A buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the tbGetBuffer function in xkb/xkbtext.c. The function uses a static buffer and a shared index (tbNext) to allocate memory, but fails to properly manage the index across multiple allocations. An attacker can trigger this vulnerability by supplying crafted XKB data that results in a series of tbGetBuffer calls, causing the index to exceed the buffer's boundaries and leading to an out-of-bounds write.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 Insufficient bounds checking on the shared buffer index (tbNext) allows it to exceed the static buffer's size.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
X server (XKB data parsing)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
 Crafted XKB configuration data
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static char *
tbGetBuffer(unsigned size)
{
    char *rtrn;

    if (size >= BUFFER_SIZE)
        return NULL;
    if ((BUFFER_SIZE - tbNext) <= size)
        tbNext = 0;
    rtrn = &textBuffer[tbNext];
    tbNext += size;
    return rtrn;
}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Crafted XKB configuration data

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write to static buffer

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is highly controllable as an attacker can manipulate the size and number of allocations to write out-of-bounds at a predictable offset relative to a static buffer.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
NA

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static char *
tbGetBuffer(unsigned size)
{
    struct textBuffer *tb;

    tb = &textBuffer[textBufferIndex];
    textBufferIndex = (textBufferIndex + 1) % NUM_BUFFER;

    if (size > tb->size) {
        free(tb->buffer);
        tb->buffer = xnfalloc(size);
        tb->size = size;
    }
    return tb->buffer;
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
 The patch replaces the single shared static buffer with a ring of multiple, individually resizable buffers. This change prevents out-of-bounds writes by ensuring each requested size is accommodated by a dynamically allocated buffer, eliminating the flawed shared index logic.
[Optional] Patch reference:
 Commit 94f11ca5cf011ef123bd222cabeaef6f424d76ac
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
The fix completely refactored the memory allocation logic from a flawed static buffer system to a safer ring of dynamic buffers. - Field: PatchType: logic refactor
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Audit other parts of the X server codebase for similar static buffer and shared index patterns.
- Fuzz the XKB parser and related string generation functions to discover potential memory corruption issues.
- Review patches applied to downstream forks of the X server to ensure this fix has been propagated.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other similar buffer management flaws may exist in the X server codebase.

