files.source/pdf/pdf-stream.c:
static fz_stream *
build_filter_chain(fz_context *ctx, fz_stream *chain, pdf_document *doc, pdf_obj *fs, pdf_obj *ps, int num, int gen, fz_compression_params *params)
{
	pdf_obj *f;
	pdf_obj *p;
	int i, n;

	fz_var(chain);

	fz_try(ctx)
	{
		n = pdf_array_len(ctx, fs);
		for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
		{
			fz_stream *chain2;

			f = pdf_array_get(ctx, fs, i);
			p = pdf_array_get(ctx, ps, i);
			chain2 = chain;
			chain = NULL;
			chain = build_filter(ctx, chain2, doc, f, p, num, gen, (i == n-1 ? params : NULL));
		}
	}
	fz_catch(ctx)
	{
		fz_drop_stream(ctx, chain);
		fz_rethrow(ctx);
	}

	return chain;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-15369

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-15369 / CWE-416:
Use After Free in build_filter_chain

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-10-16 — Patch release: 2017-09-25

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
MuPDF versions prior to commit c2663e51238ec8256da7fc61ad580db891d9fe9a

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-416: Use After Free
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `build_filter_chain` function in MuPDF. During exception handling, the `chain` pointer, which is expected to be NULL in the `fz_catch` block, can have its stale value restored from a register due to compiler optimizations. This leads to a call to `fz_drop_stream` on a dangling pointer, causing a use-after-free condition.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Compiler optimization of the `chain` pointer across a `setjmp`/`longjmp` boundary, causing a stale pointer value to be used in an exception handler.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
PDF file parsing
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Use-after-free leading to memory corruption
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Maliciously crafted PDF file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

```c
fz_try(ctx)
{
    n = pdf_array_len(ctx, fs);
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        fz_stream *chain2;

        f = pdf_array_get(ctx, fs, i);
        p = pdf_array_get(ctx, ps, i);
        chain2 = chain;
        chain = NULL;
        chain = build_filter(ctx, chain2, doc, f, p, num, gen, (i == n-1 ? params : NULL));
    }
}
fz_catch(ctx)
{
    fz_drop_stream(ctx, chain);
    fz_rethrow(ctx);
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Maliciously crafted PDF file

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Use-after-free (UAF) primitive via `fz_drop_stream` on a dangling pointer.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
Exploitability is medium, as it depends on specific compiler optimizations and requires a crafted PDF to trigger the exception.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
Not available in input

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
static fz_stream *
build_filter_chain(fz_context *ctx, fz_stream *chain, pdf_document *doc, pdf_obj *fs, pdf_obj *ps, int num, int gen, fz_compression_params *params)
{
    pdf_obj *f;
    pdf_obj *p;
    int i, n;

    fz_var(chain);

    fz_try(ctx)
    {
        n = pdf_array_len(ctx, fs);
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            fz_stream *chain2;

            f = pdf_array_get(ctx, fs, i);
            p = pdf_array_get(ctx, ps, i);
            chain2 = chain;
            chain = NULL;
            chain = build_filter(ctx, chain2, doc, f, p, num, gen, (i == n-1 ? params : NULL));
        }
    }
    fz_catch(ctx)
    {
        fz_drop_stream(ctx, chain);
        fz_rethrow(ctx);
    }

    return chain;
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds `fz_var(chain);` to prevent the compiler from optimizing the `chain` variable into a register, ensuring its value remains NULL in the exception handler as intended.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/ArtifexSoftware/mupdf/commit/c2663e51238ec8256da7fc61ad580db891d9fe9a
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (Compiler directive added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the codebase that use `fz_try`/`fz_catch` and modify local pointers that are later used in the catch block.
- Audit the use of `setjmp`/`longjmp` patterns for similar optimization-related issues.
- Fuzz PDF parsing specifically targeting filter chains to trigger exceptions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar use-after-free bugs may exist in other functions using MuPDF's exception handling without `fz_var`.

