annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-15385

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-15385 / CWE-119: ELF version parsing crash

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-10-16 — Patch release: 2017-10-14

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
radare2 before commit 21a6f570ba33fa9f52f1bba87f07acc4e8c178f4

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
A vulnerability exists in the `store_versioninfo_gnu_verdef` function in `libr/bin/format/elf/elf.c` when processing ELF version definition sections. The bug is triggered by a specially crafted ELF file where the `vd_next` field of a `Verdef` structure is manipulated to cause an integer underflow of the loop index `i`. This leads to an out-of-bounds memory access, making the issue exploitable for memory corruption.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The loop condition did not check if the loop index `i` could become negative, allowing an integer underflow when processing the `vd_next` field.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
ELF file parser
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Memory corruption -> Potential RCE
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious ELF file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
	for (cnt = 0, i = 0; cnt < shdr->sh_info && ((char *)defs + i < end); ++cnt) {
		Sdb *sdb_verdef = sdb_new0 ();
		char *vstart = ((char*)defs) + i;
		char key[32] = {0};
		Elf_(Verdef) *verdef = (Elf_(Verdef)*)vstart;
		// ...
		if (!verdef->vd_next) {
			sdb_free (sdb_verdef);
			goto out_error;
		}
		i += verdef->vd_next;
	}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Parsing a malicious ELF file

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Heap buffer underflow/overflow

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability provides a controlled out-of-bounds memory access primitive, which can be leveraged to achieve memory corruption and potentially code execution, depending on the memory layout of the target process.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft an ELF file with a `.gnu.version` section containing a `Verdef` entry.
- Set the `vd_next` field of the `Verdef` entry to a large positive value.
- When `radare2` parses this file, the loop index `i` will underflow and become a large negative number.
- This causes the pointer `vstart` to point to memory before the allocated `defs` buffer.
- Subsequent memory reads/writes using this pointer lead to an out-of-bounds access, resulting in memory corruption.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
	for (cnt = 0, i = 0; i >= 0 && cnt < shdr->sh_info && ((char *)defs + i < end); ++cnt) {
		// ...
		if (!verdef->vd_next) {
			sdb_free (sdb_verdef);
			goto out_error;
		}
		if ((st32)verdef->vd_next < 1) {
			eprintf ("Warning: Invalid vd_next in the ELF version\n");
			break;
		}
		i += verdef->vd_next;
	}
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a condition to the loop to ensure the index `i` is non-negative and adds a check to break the loop if `verdef->vd_next` is zero or negative, preventing the integer underflow.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/radareorg/radare2/commit/21a6f570ba33fa9f52f1bba87f07acc4e8c178f4
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other ELF parsing loops in radare2 for similar integer underflow/overflow issues where loop counters are derived from file-parsed values.
- Fuzz the ELF parser, specifically targeting fields used as offsets or loop counters like `vd_next`.
- Audit other binary format parsers within the radare2 project for analogous patterns of using attacker-controlled data in loop conditions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar logic errors in other file parsers could lead to comparable memory corruption vulnerabilities.

