[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-12987

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-12987 / CWE-125:
Out-of-bounds read in TIM IE processing

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-09-14 — Patch release: 2017-09-14

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
tcpdump versions prior to commit 99798bd9a41bd3d03fdc1e949810a38967f20ed3

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `parse_elements` function when processing an IEEE 802.11 TIM Information Element. It is triggered by a crafted packet where the TIM element's length is greater than 3. The vulnerability is exploitable because the arguments to `memcpy()` are incorrect, causing data to be read from an incorrect source address (`p + (tim.length - 3)`) instead of the intended location (`p + offset + 3`), leading to an out-of-bounds read.

[Mandatory] Root cause:
Incorrect arguments passed to `memcpy()`, using the element length as an offset from the packet start instead of from the current parsing position.

[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network packet parser (tcpdump)

[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of service via application crash (buffer over-read)

[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Crafted 802.11 frame with a TIM Information Element

[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
		case E_TIM:
			memcpy(&tim, p + offset, 2);
			offset += 2;
			length -= 2;
			if (tim.length <= 3) {
				offset += tim.length;
				length -= tim.length;
				break;
			}
			if (tim.length - 3 > (int)sizeof tim.bitmap)
				return 0;
			memcpy(&tim.count, p + offset, 3);
			offset += 3;
			length -= 3;

			memcpy(tim.bitmap, p + (tim.length - 3),
			    (tim.length - 3));
			offset += tim.length - 3;
			length -= tim.length - 3;

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
802.11 frame capture file or live network interface

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read (out-of-bounds)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can cause an out-of-bounds read by sending a specially crafted 802.11 frame, potentially leading to a crash or information disclosure.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft an 802.11 management frame.
- Include a TIM Information Element with a length greater than 3.
- Ensure the packet is processed by a vulnerable version of tcpdump.
- The incorrect `memcpy` call will read data from an out-of-bounds location.
- This may cause tcpdump to crash or leak memory contents.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
		case E_TIM:
			memcpy(&tim, p + offset, 2);
			offset += 2;
			length -= 2;
			if (tim.length <= 3) {
				offset += tim.length;
				length -= tim.length;
				break;
			}
			if (tim.length - 3 > (int)sizeof tim.bitmap)
				return 0;
			memcpy(&tim.count, p + offset, 3);
			offset += 3;
			length -= 3;

			memcpy(tim.bitmap, p + offset + 3, tim.length - 3);
			offset += tim.length - 3;
			length -= tim.length - 3;

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch corrected the source address argument in the `memcpy` call from `p + (tim.length - 3)` to `p + offset + 3`, ensuring data is read from the correct position within the packet buffer.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit 99798bd9a41bd3d03fdc1e949810a38967f20ed3

[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- Field: PatchType (logic refactor)

[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other `memcpy` calls in the `parse_elements` function for similar incorrect offset calculations.
- Fuzz the 802.11 parser with malformed elements to uncover any other potential out-of-bounds reads or writes.
- Diff against related network protocol parsers to ensure they do not share the same flawed logic.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Incorrect pointer arithmetic in other element parsers could lead to similar out-of-bounds read vulnerabilities.

