		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP RPY"));
	else if (l_strnstart(ndo, "ERR ", 4, (const char *)bp, length))
		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP ERR"));
	else if (l_strnstart(ndo, "ANS ", 4, (const char *)bp, length))
		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP ANS"));
	else if (l_strnstart(ndo, "NUL ", 4, (const char *)bp, length))
		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP NUL"));
	else if (l_strnstart(ndo, "SEQ ", 4, (const char *)bp, length))
		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP SEQ"));
	else if (l_strnstart(ndo, "END", 4, (const char *)bp, length))
		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP END"));
	else
		ND_PRINT((ndo, " BEEP (payload or undecoded)"));
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network packet parsing (BEEP protocol)
[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Buffer over-read
[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
Exploitable by sending a specially crafted BEEP packet with insufficient length to trigger an out-of-bounds read.
[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft a BEEP packet shorter than the expected 4-byte string prefix.
- Send the packet to a target running tcpdump with BEEP parsing enabled.
- Trigger the `l_strnstart` call without proper bounds checking.
- Cause tcpdump to read past the buffer boundary.
- Potentially crash the application or leak memory contents.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
