files.print-lldp.c:
static int
lldp_mgmt_addr_tlv_print(netdissect_options *ndo,
                         const u_char *pptr, u_int len)
{
    uint8_t mgmt_addr_len, intf_num_subtype, oid_len;
    const u_char *tptr;
    u_int tlen;
    char *mgmt_addr;

    tlen = len;
    tptr = pptr;

    if (tlen < 1) {
        return 0;
    }
    mgmt_addr_len = *tptr++;
    tlen--;

    if (tlen < mgmt_addr_len) {
        return 0;
    }

    mgmt_addr = lldp_network_addr_print(ndo, tptr, mgmt_addr_len);
    if (mgmt_addr == NULL) {
        return 0;
    }
    ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  Management Address length %u, %s",
           mgmt_addr_len, mgmt_addr));
    tptr += mgmt_addr_len;
    tlen -= mgmt_addr_len;

    if (tlen < LLDP_INTF_NUM_LEN) {
        return 0;
    }

    intf_num_subtype = *tptr;
    ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  %s Interface Numbering (%u): %u",
           tok2str(lldp_intf_numb_subtype_values, "Unknown", intf_num_subtype),
           intf_num_subtype,
           EXTRACT_32BITS(tptr + 1)));

    tptr += LLDP_INTF_NUM_LEN;
    tlen -= LLDP_INTF_NUM_LEN;

    /*
     * The OID is optional.
     */
    if (tlen) {
        oid_len = *tptr;

        if (tlen < 1U + oid_len) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (oid_len) {
            ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  OID length %u", oid_len));
            safeputs(ndo, tptr + 1, oid_len);
        }
    }

    return 1;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID: CVE-2017-13027

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-13027 / CWE-125:
Out-of-bounds read

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-09-14 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `lldp_mgmt_addr_tlv_print` function in `print-lldp.c`. It is triggered when processing an LLDP Management Address TLV that contains an optional OID. The function fails to correctly account for the length byte of the OID itself when performing a bounds check, leading to a potential out-of-bounds read. This is exploitable because the `oid_len` value from the packet is trusted without sufficient validation, as shown in the code excerpt.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The bounds check `if (tlen < oid_len)` does not account for the one-byte length field preceding the OID data.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network packet parser (tcpdump)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Information leak via out-of-bounds read
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malformed LLDP packet with a crafted OID TLV
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

    if (tlen) {
        oid_len = *tptr;

        if (tlen < oid_len) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (oid_len) {
            ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  OID length %u", oid_len));
            safeputs(ndo, tptr + 1, oid_len);
        }
    }

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network packet parsing via tcpdump
[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read (out-of-bounds)
[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is easily triggered by sending a specially crafted network packet to be parsed by an affected version of tcpdump.
[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Not available in input

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:

    if (tlen) {
        oid_len = *tptr;

        if (tlen < 1U + oid_len) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (oid_len) {
            ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  OID length %u", oid_len));
            safeputs(ndo, tptr + 1, oid_len);
        }
    }

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch corrects the bounds check to ensure the remaining buffer length `tlen` is sufficient for both the OID length byte and the OID data itself by changing the condition to `if (tlen < 1U + oid_len)`.
[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump/commit/a77ff09c46560bc895dea11dc9fe643486b056ac
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other TLV parsers in the LLDP module for similar incorrect bounds checks.
- Fuzz the entire LLDP parser with varied packet lengths and fields.
- Audit other protocol dissectors in the project for patterns of `tlen < field_len` without accounting for the field's own size.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds reads may exist in other protocol parsers with analogous logic.

