[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-13037

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-13037 / CWE-125:
Buffer over-read

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: NA — Patch release: NA

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
NA

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `ip_printts` function within `print-ip.c`, which processes timestamp options in IP packets. The function reads packet data without sufficient bounds checks before accessing `cp[2]`, `cp[3]`, and subsequent offsets within the loop, leading to a buffer over-read. This can be triggered by crafting a packet with a length field that causes the function to read past the end of the packet buffer.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The function reads packet data without sufficient bounds checks before accessing `cp[2]`, `cp[3]`, and subsequent offsets within the loop.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network packet parser (tcpdump)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service or potential information disclosure via buffer over-read.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A specially crafted network packet containing IP timestamp options.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
static int
ip_printts(netdissect_options *ndo,
           register const u_char *cp, u_int length)
{
...
	if (length < 4) {
		ND_PRINT((ndo, "[bad length %u]", length));
		return (0);
	}
	ND_PRINT((ndo, " TS{"));
	hoplen = ((cp[3]&0xF) != IPOPT_TS_TSONLY) ? 8 : 4;
	if ((length - 4) & (hoplen-1))
		ND_PRINT((ndo, "[bad length %u]", length));
	ptr = cp[2] - 1;
...
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network packet with IP timestamp options.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read (out-of-bounds).

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger the over-read by sending a malicious packet that tcpdump processes, potentially leading to a crash or information leak.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft an IP packet with a timestamp option.
- Set the length and pointer fields to cause reads beyond the packet buffer.
- Send the packet to a target where tcpdump is running with appropriate privileges.
- tcpdump processes the packet, triggering the over-read.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
static int
ip_printts(netdissect_options *ndo,
           register const u_char *cp, u_int length)
{
...
	if (length < 4) {
