[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-13039

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-13039 / CWE-125:
Out-of-bounds read

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-09-14 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `ikev1_attr_print` function within `print-isakmp.c`. It can be triggered when processing a crafted ISAKMP packet where the length fields indicate a size that extends beyond the end of the packet buffer. The function reads data from this out-of-bounds memory to print attribute values, which can lead to an information leak or a crash. This occurs because the code calculates `totlen` from packet data but does not sufficiently check if the subsequent reads from `&p[2]` and `&p[4]` are within the valid buffer bounds before passing them to `rawprint`.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Insufficient bounds checking on packet-supplied length fields before reading from the packet buffer.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network service (packet capture parser)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Information leak or denial of service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Crafted ISAKMP packet
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

```c
	if (p[0] & 0x80)
		totlen = 4;
	else {
		ND_TCHECK_16BITS(&p[2]);
		totlen = 4 + EXTRACT_16BITS(&p[2]);
	}
	if (ep2 < p + totlen) {
		ND_PRINT((ndo,"[|attr]"));
		return ep2 + 1;
	}
	...
	} else {
		ND_PRINT((ndo,"len=%d value=", totlen - 4));
		if (!rawprint(ndo, (const uint8_t *)&p[4], totlen - 4)) {
			ND_PRINT((ndo,")"));
			goto trunc;
		}
	}
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Crafted network packet parsed by tcpdump (ISAKMP dissector)

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read, out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is controlled by the attacker, who can craft a packet to trigger an out-of-bounds read, potentially leaking memory or causing a crash.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker crafts an ISAKMP packet.
- The packet's attribute length field is set to a value that causes `totlen` to be larger than the remaining packet data.
- The packet is sent to a machine or processed by a tool running tcpdump.
- `ikev1_attr_print` processes the packet and fails to adequately validate the length before calling `rawprint`.
- `rawprint` reads data past the end of the allocated packet buffer.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
	ND_TCHECK(p[0]);
	if (p[0] & 0x80)
		totlen = 4;
	else {
		ND_TCHECK_16BITS(&p[2]);
		totlen = 4 + EXTRACT_16BITS(&p[2]);
	}
	if (ep2 < p + totlen) {
		ND_PRINT((ndo,"[|attr]"));
		return ep2 + 1;
	}

	ND_TCHECK_16BITS(&p[0]);
	...
	} else {
		ND_PRINT((ndo,"len=%d value=", totlen - 4));
		if (!rawprint(ndo, (const uint8_t *)&p[4], totlen - 4)) {
			ND_PRINT((ndo,")"));
			goto trunc;
		}
	}

trunc:
	return NULL;
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix adds explicit bounds checks (`ND_TCHECK`) before reading from the packet buffer and ensures that calls to `rawprint` are checked for failure, returning NULL on truncation to signal an error to the caller.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump/commit/e0a5a02b0fc1900a69d6c37ed0aab36fb8494e6d
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
Bounds check added.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other packet parsers in the tcpdump codebase for similar missing `ND_TCHECK` calls before buffer reads.
- Fuzz the ISAKMP dissector and related parsers with malformed packets to discover any remaining out-of-bounds reads.
- Diff forks of tcpdump to ensure they have incorporated these bounds checks.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other dissectors in tcpdump may have similar out-of-bounds read vulnerabilities.

