            hexdump = TRUE;
            break;
        }
        ND_PRINT((ndo, ", %s", ip6addr_string(ndo, tptr + 1)));
        break;
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network packet parsing via tcpdump

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger the vulnerability by sending a specially crafted packet with a CFM Sender ID TLV that has an insufficient length, causing tcpdump to read past the end of the packet buffer.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft a packet containing a CFM Sender ID TLV.
- Set the Network Address Type to AFNUM_INET or AFNUM_INET6.
- Set the total TLV length to be less than required (e.g., less than 5 for IPv4 or 17 for IPv6).
- Send the packet to a machine running tcpdump to capture traffic.
- tcpdump will crash when attempting to read the address data.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
static int
cfm_network_addr_print(netdissect_options *ndo,
                       register const u_char *tptr, const u_int length)
{
    u_int network_addr_type;
    u_int hexdump =  FALSE;

    /*
     * Altough AFIs are tpically 2 octects wide,
     * 802.1ab specifies that this field width
     * is only once octet
     */
    if (length < 1) {
        ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  Network Address Type (invalid, no data"));
        return hexdump;
    }
    /* The calling function must make any due ND_TCHECK calls. */
    network_addr_type = *tptr;
    ND_PRINT((ndo, "\n\t  Network Address Type %s (%u)",
           tok2str(af_values, "Unknown", network_addr_type),
           network_addr_type));

    /*
     * Resolve the passed in Address.
     */
    switch(network_addr_type) {
    case AFNUM_INET:
        if (length != 1 + 4) {
            ND_PRINT((ndo, "(invalid IPv4 address length %u)", length - 1));
            hexdump = TRUE;
            break;
        }
        ND_PRINT((ndo, ", %s", ipaddr_string(ndo, tptr + 1)));
        break;

    case AFNUM_INET6:
        if (length != 1 + 16) {
            ND_PRINT((ndo, "(invalid IPv6 address length %u)", length - 1));
            hexdump = TRUE;
            break;
        }
        ND_PRINT((ndo, ", %s", ip6addr_string(ndo, tptr + 1)));
        break;

    default:
        hexdump = TRUE;
        break;
    }

    return hexdump;
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a `length` parameter to the function and uses it to validate that the input buffer is large enough to contain the address type and the subsequent address data before attempting to read it.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump/commit/5d340a5ca6e420a70297cdbdf777333f18bfdab7
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other packet parsing functions in tcpdump that may lack similar length checks.
- Fuzz related parsers, especially those handling TLV structures, to discover similar out-of-bounds reads.
- Diff forks of tcpdump to see if they have merged this fix or remain vulnerable.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds read vulnerabilities may exist in other protocol parsers.

END INSTRUCTION.

