annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-13690

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-13690 / CWE-125:
Buffer over-read in IKEv2 payload parsing

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-09-14 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump (commit 8dca25d26c7ca2caf6138267f6f17111212c156e and prior)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug exists in the `ikev2_gen_print` function within `print-isakmp.c`. It is triggered when processing a crafted IKEv2 packet where the `len` field of the `isakmp_gen` structure is less than 4. The code calculates `ntohs(e.len) - 4` and passes this result to `rawprint`. If `ntohs(e.len)` is smaller than 4, this calculation underflows, resulting in a large size_t value that causes `rawprint` to read data past the end of the packet buffer, leading to an out-of-bounds read.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Insufficient validation of the `len` field, which allows an underflow when calculating the payload length.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network packet parser (tcpdump)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Information leak / potential denial of service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Crafted IKEv2 packet sent over the network and captured by tcpdump.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static const u_char *
ikev2_gen_print(netdissect_options *ndo, u_char tpay,
		const struct isakmp_gen *ext)
{
	struct isakmp_gen e;

	ND_TCHECK(*ext);
	UNALIGNED_MEMCPY(&e, ext, sizeof(e));
	ikev2_pay_print(ndo, NPSTR(tpay), e.critical);

	ND_PRINT((ndo," len=%d", ntohs(e.len) - 4));
	if (2 < ndo->ndo_vflag && 4 < ntohs(e.len)) {
		/* Print the entire payload in hex */
		ND_PRINT((ndo," "));
		if (!rawprint(ndo, (const uint8_t *)(ext + 1), ntohs(e.len) - 4))
			goto trunc;
	}
	return (const u_char *)ext + ntohs(e.len);
trunc:
	ND_PRINT((ndo," [|%s]", NPSTR(tpay)));
	return NULL;
}

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network packet capture

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read (out-of-bounds)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is easy to trigger with a single crafted packet, allowing an attacker to read memory contents adjacent to the captured packet buffer.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create an IKEv2 packet with an `isakmp_gen` header.
- Set the `len` field in the header to a value less than 4 (e.g., 3).
- Transmit the packet on the network.
- Run tcpdump with verbose flags (`-v` or `-vv`) to capture and process the packet.
- The `ikev2_gen_print` function will be invoked, leading to an out-of-bounds read.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
ND_TCHECK2(*ext, sizeof(e));
UNALIGNED_MEMCPY(&e, ext, sizeof(e));
ikev2_pay_print(ndo, NPSTR(tpay), e.critical);

ND_PRINT((ndo," len=%d", ntohs(e.len) - 4));
if (2 < ndo->ndo_vflag && 4 < ntohs(e.len)) {
	/* Print the entire payload in hex */
	ND_PRINT((ndo," "));
	if (!rawprint(ndo, (const uint8_t *)(ext + 1), ntohs(e.len) - 4))
		goto trunc;
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch replaces the `ND_TCHECK(*ext)` macro with `ND_TCHECK2(*ext, sizeof(e))` to ensure that the entire `isakmp_gen` structure is present in the packet buffer before it is copied and used, preventing the out-of-bounds read.
[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump/commit/8dca25d26c7ca2caf6138267f6f17111212c156e
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other packet parsing functions in tcpdump that use `ND_TCHECK` on single members of a struct instead of the full struct size.
- Fuzz the IKEv1 and IKEv2 parsers with packets containing truncated headers and incorrect length fields.
- Diff against other network monitoring tools that implement similar protocol parsers to identify shared logic.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar bounds-checking issues could exist in other protocol parsers within tcpdump.

