[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-14501

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-14501 / CWE-125:
Out-of-bounds read

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: [NA] — Patch release: [NA]

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
[NA]

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `parse_file_info` function within `libarchive/archive_read_support_format_iso9660.c`. The function reads the length of a directory record from the input buffer (`isodirrec`) without first validating that the supplied buffer size (`reclen`) is sufficient to contain that record. This allows an out-of-bounds read of one byte when `reclen` is less than the value at `DR_length_offset`. The vulnerability is triggered by processing a crafted ISO 9660 image where the directory record's length field exceeds the actual size of the record provided to the function.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The function reads a length field from an untrusted input buffer without verifying that the buffer is large enough to contain that length.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
File parser (ISO 9660 archive)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Out-of-bounds read
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Crafted ISO 9660 image file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

static struct file_info *
parse_file_info(struct archive_read *a, struct file_info *parent,
    const unsigned char *isodirrec, size_t reclen)
{
	struct iso9660 *iso9660;
	struct file_info *file, *filep;
	size_t name_len;
	const unsigned char *rr_start, *rr_end;
	const unsigned char *p;
	size_t dr_len;
	uint64_t fsize, offset;
	int32_t location;
	int flags;

	iso9660 = (struct iso9660 *)(a->format->data);

	dr_len = (size_t)isodirrec[DR_length_offset];
	name_len = (size_t)isodirrec[DR_name_len_offset];

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File format

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is triggered by a maliciously crafted ISO file, leading to a controlled out-of-bounds read.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker creates a malicious ISO 9660 image.
- The image contains a directory record where the length byte at `DR_length_offset` is set to a value larger than the record's actual size.
- The application processes the image using the vulnerable libarchive.
- `parse_file_info` is called with a buffer `reclen` smaller than the specified `dr_len`.
- The function reads `isodirrec[DR_length_offset]` out of bounds.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static struct file_info *
parse_file_info(struct archive_read *a, struct file_info *parent,
    const unsigned char *isodirrec, size_t reclen)
{
	struct iso9660 *iso9660;
	struct file_info *file, *filep;
	size_t name_len;
	const unsigned char *rr_start, *rr_end;
	const unsigned char *p;
	size_t dr_len;
	uint64_t fsize, offset;
	int32_t location;
	int flags;

	iso9660 = (struct iso9660 *)(a->format->data);

	if (reclen == 0 || reclen < (dr_len = (size_t)isodirrec[DR_length_offset])) {
		archive_set_error(&a->archive, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_MISC,
			"Invalid directory record length");
		return (NULL);
	}
	name_len = (size_t)isodirrec[DR_name_len_offset];

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a check to ensure the input buffer size `reclen` is greater than or equal to the directory record length (`dr_len`) read from the buffer. If the check fails, the function returns an error, preventing the out-of-bounds read.
[Optional] Patch reference:
[NA]
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other parsers in libarchive that read length fields from untrusted input to ensure similar bounds checks exist.
- Fuzz the ISO 9660 parser with varied directory record lengths and buffer sizes to find related issues.
- Diff forks or older versions of libarchive to check if this vulnerability was introduced elsewhere.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds reads may exist in other format parsers lacking input validation.

END INSTRUCTION.

