files.Source/C++/Core/Ap4StszAtom.cpp:
AP4_StszAtom::AP4_StszAtom(AP4_UI32        size, 
                           AP4_UI08        version,
                           AP4_UI32        flags,
                           AP4_ByteStream& stream) :
    AP4_Atom(AP4_ATOM_TYPE_STSZ, size, version, flags)
{
    stream.ReadUI32(m_SampleSize);
    stream.ReadUI32(m_SampleCount);
    if (m_SampleSize == 0) { // means that the samples have different sizes
        // check for overflow
        if (m_SampleCount > (size-8)/4) {
            m_SampleCount = 0;
            return;
        }

        // read the entries
        AP4_Cardinal sample_count = m_SampleCount;
        m_Entries.SetItemCount(sample_count);
        unsigned char* buffer = new unsigned char[sample_count*4];
        AP4_Result result = stream.Read(buffer, sample_count*4);
        if (AP4_FAILED(result)) {
            delete[] buffer;
            return;
        }
        for (unsigned int i=0; i<sample_count; i++) {
            m_Entries[i] = AP4_BytesToUInt32BE(&buffer[i*4]);
        }
        delete[] buffer;
    }
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2017-14643

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2017-14643 / CWE-125:
Out-of-bounds Read

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2017-09-21 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the AP4_StszAtom constructor within Bento4. When parsing a media file, if the `m_SampleSize` field is zero, the code proceeds to read an array of sample sizes from the input stream. The original code did not validate that the `m_SampleCount` was consistent with the overall atom `size`, potentially allowing a read operation beyond the bounds of the allocated buffer, leading to an out-of-bounds read.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The code failed to validate the `m_SampleCount` field against the total atom `size` before allocating a buffer and reading data.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Media file parser, specifically when processing malformed MP4 files.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service (via application crash)
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Specially crafted MP4 file with a `stsz` atom containing an inconsistent `sample_count` value.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```cpp
    stream.ReadUI32(m_SampleSize);
    stream.ReadUI32(m_SampleCount);
    if (m_SampleSize == 0) { // means that the samples have different sizes
        // check for overflow
        if (m_SampleCount > (size-8)/4) {
            m_SampleCount = 0;
            return;
        }

        // read the entries
        AP4_Cardinal sample_count = m_SampleCount;
        m_Entries.SetItemCount(sample_count);
        unsigned char* buffer = new unsigned char[sample_count*4];
        AP4_Result result = stream.Read(buffer, sample_count*4);
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File parsing via MP4 `stsz` atom.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger the vulnerability by crafting a file where the `sample_count` field in the `stsz` atom is larger than the remaining atom size allows, leading to a read past the end of a heap-allocated buffer.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create an MP4 file with a `stsz` atom.
- Set the `sample_size` field to 0 to indicate variable sample sizes.
- Set the `sample_count` field to a value that, when multiplied by 4, exceeds the atom's size minus the header.
- The application will allocate a buffer based on `sample_count` but attempt to read more data than is available in the atom, causing an out-of-bounds read.
- This can lead to a crash or information disclosure.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```cpp
    stream.ReadUI32(m_SampleSize);
    stream.ReadUI32(m_SampleCount);
    if (m_SampleSize == 0) { // means that the samples have different sizes
        // check for overflow
        if (m_SampleCount > (size-8)/4) {
            m_SampleCount = 0;
            return;
        }

        // read the entries
        AP4_Cardinal sample_count = m_SampleCount;
        m_Entries.SetItemCount(sample_count);
        unsigned char* buffer = new unsigned char[sample_count*4];
        AP4_Result result = stream.Read(buffer, sample_count*4);
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a check to ensure that `m_SampleCount` does not represent more data entries than can fit within the atom's size, preventing an out-of-bounds read.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/axiomatic-systems/Bento4/commit/5eb8cf89d724ccb0b4ce5f24171ec7c11f0a7647
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other atom parsers in the Bento4 library for similar integer overflow or bounds-checking issues where a count field is used to allocate memory or read data.
- Fuzz the MP4 parser with atoms containing inconsistent size and count fields to identify similar latent vulnerabilities.
- Audit any code that performs memory allocation based on values parsed from untrusted input.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other atom parsers may have similar bounds-checking flaws leading to potential crashes.

