Image file processing via the timg utility.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Heap-based out-of-bounds write.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is triggered by processing an image that results in uneven column widths during quarter-rendering, allowing an attacker to corrupt the heap.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Provide an image or set of terminal dimensions that cause quarter-rendering.
- Ensure the calculated canvas width is an odd number.
- The application will allocate a buffer that is too small.
- Subsequent writes to the buffer will overflow, corrupting adjacent heap metadata.
- This corruption can be leveraged to achieve arbitrary code execution.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
    // For quarter, we have one extra possible pixel wider.
    const size_t new_backing = (width + 1) * (height + 1) * sizeof(rgba_t);
    if (new_backing > backing_buffer_size_) {
        backing_buffer_      = (rgba_t *)realloc(backing_buffer_, new_backing);
        backing_buffer_size_ = new_backing;
    }

    const size_t new_empty = (width + 1) * sizeof(rgba_t);
    if (new_empty > empty_line_size_) {
        empty_line_      = (rgba_t *)realloc(empty_line_, new_empty);
        empty_line_size_ = new_empty;
        memset(empty_line_, 0x00, empty_line_size_);
    }
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch corrects the buffer size calculation by adding 1 to the width in both `new_backing` and `new_empty` allocations, ensuring sufficient space for overhang pixels.

