The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 Out-of-bounds Write
[Mandatory] Summary:
 A heap buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the parseOperand function in libr/asm/p/asm_x86_nz.c. It is triggered when parsing assembly operands with more than two registers in a memory expression (e.g., `[reg1+reg2+reg3]`). The code increments a `reg_index` counter without checking bounds before using it to write to the fixed-size `op->regs` and `op->scale` arrays, leading to an out-of-bounds write.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 Unchecked increment of reg_index used as an array index.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Assembly string parser (x86 assembler)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service (Crash)
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
 Crafted assembly instruction string
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
if (str[pos] == '+' || str[pos] == '-' || str[pos] == ']') {
    if (reg != X86R_UNDEFINED) {
        op->regs[reg_index] = reg;
        op->scale[reg_index] = temp;
    }
    ++reg_index;
} else {
    op->offset += temp;
    op->regs[reg_index] = X86R_UNDEFINED;
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
API for parsing assembly strings

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Heap out-of-bounds write

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The offset and value of the out-of-bounds write are controlled by the crafted assembly string, making it a reliable primitive.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft an assembly instruction string with a memory operand (e.g., `[... ]`).
- Inside the brackets, include more than two registers separated by `+` or `-` operators.
- This causes the `reg_index` variable to be incremented past the size of the `op->regs` array (which is 2).
- The subsequent write to `op->regs[reg_index]` or `op->scale[reg_index]` corrupts adjacent heap memory.
- This memory corruption leads to a crash (Denial of Service) and may potentially lead to code execution.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
if (reg != X86R_UNDEFINED) {
    if (reg_index < 2) {
        op->regs[reg_index] = reg;
        op->scale[reg_index] = temp;
    }
    ++reg_index;
} else {
    op->offset += temp;
    if (reg_index < 2) {
        op->regs[reg_index] = X86R_UNDEFINED;
    }
}
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
 The patch adds bounds checks (reg_index < 2) before writing to the op->regs and op->scale arrays.
