[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2018-20458 / CWE-125: oobread crash in dyldcache

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2018-12-25 — Patch release: 2018-12-02

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
radare2 (pre-commit 30f4c7b52a)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `r_bin_dyldcache_from_bytes_new` function in `libr/bin/format/mach0/dyldcache.c`. It is triggered when parsing a specially crafted or truncated dyldcache file. The function calls `r_buf_set_bytes` without first verifying that the preceding call to `r_buf_new` was successful, leading to a potential out-of-bounds read and application crash.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The root cause is a missing NULL check on the return value of `r_buf_new` before it is used in `r_buf_set_bytes`.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
File parser (dyldcache)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service (crash)
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Truncated dyldcache file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t* r_bin_dyldcache_from_bytes_new(const ut8* buf, ut64 size) {
	struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t *bin;
	if (!(bin = malloc (sizeof (struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t)))) {
		return NULL;
	}
	memset (bin, 0, sizeof (struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t));
	if (!buf) {
		return r_bin_dyldcache_free (bin);
	}
	bin->b = r_buf_new();
	if (!r_buf_set_bytes (bin->b, buf, size)) {
		return r_bin_dyldcache_free (bin);
	}
	if (!r_bin_dyldcache_init (bin)) {
		return r_bin_dyldcache_free (bin);
	}
	bin->size = size;
	return bin;
}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File parser (dyldcache)

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Denial of Service (crash)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger the crash by providing a malicious dyldcache file, leading to a reliable denial of service with no further attacker control.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a malformed or truncated dyldcache file.
- Pass the file to an application using the vulnerable radare2 library for parsing.
- The `r_bin_dyldcache_from_bytes_new` function will be called during processing.
- If `r_buf_new` fails, `r_buf_set_bytes` will be called with a NULL pointer, causing a crash.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t* r_bin_dyldcache_from_bytes_new(const ut8* buf, ut64 size) {
	struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t *bin = R_NEW0 (struct r_bin_dyldcache_obj_t);
	if (!bin) {
		return NULL;
	}
	if (!buf) {
		return r_bin_dyldcache_free (bin);
	}
	bin->b = r_buf_new ();
	if (!bin->b || !r_buf_set_bytes (bin->b, buf, size)) {
		return r_bin_dyldcache_free (bin);
	}
	if (!r_bin_dyldcache_init (bin)) {
		return r_bin_dyldcache_free (bin);
	}
	bin->size = size;
	return bin;
}
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix replaces the manual `malloc` and `memset` with the `R_NEW0` macro and adds a NULL check on the `bin->b` buffer before it is passed to `r_buf_set_bytes`, preventing the crash.
[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/radareorg/radare2/commit/30f4c7b52a4e2dc0d0b1bae487d90f5437c69d19
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- PatchType: NULL check added
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the codebase that allocate memory and immediately use the pointer without checking for failure.
- Audit the usage of `r_buf_new` to ensure all call sites check its return value before use.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar NULL-dereference crashes may exist in other file parsers.

