[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-1796

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-1796 / CWE-119:
KVM buffer overflow in MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-03-22 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
A buffer overflow exists in the KVM function `kvm_set_msr_common` when handling the `MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME` MSR. A malicious guest can set the Guest Physical Address (GPA) for the time structure such that the write operation straddles a page boundary. This allows the guest to corrupt host kernel memory, as the code uses `kmap_atomic` and `memcpy` with a guest-controlled offset without proper alignment checks.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Insufficient validation of the guest-provided GPA alignment for the `MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME` write operation.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Guest virtual machine via a wrmsr instruction.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Host kernel memory corruption → Privilege escalation.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A malicious guest writes to the MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME MSR with a misaligned address.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
case MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME_NEW:
case MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME: {
	kvmclock_reset(vcpu);

	vcpu->arch.time = data;
	kvm_make_request(KVM_REQ_CLOCK_UPDATE, vcpu);

	/* we verify if the enable bit is set... */
	if (!(data & 1))
		break;

	/* ...but clean it before doing the actual write */
	vcpu->arch.time_offset = data & ~(PAGE_MASK | 1);

	vcpu->arch.time_page =
			gfn_to_page(vcpu->kvm, data >> PAGE_SHIFT);

	if (is_error_page(vcpu->arch.time_page))
		vcpu->arch.time_page = NULL;

	break;
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
wrmsr instruction for MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME from a guest VM.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary kernel memory write (controlled offset and length).

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
A malicious guest can reliably trigger the overflow by setting a misaligned GPA, leading to host memory corruption with high exploitability.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- The guest executes the `wrmsr` instruction targeting `MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME`.
- The value written includes a guest-controlled physical address that is not 32-byte aligned.
- The KVM host code calculates `time_offset` from this address without validating its alignment.
- `gfn_to_page` is called to map the page, and a subsequent `memcpy` writes the `pvclock_vcpu_time_info` structure.
- Because the address is misaligned, this write crosses a page boundary and corrupts adjacent host memory.
- This results in a controlled out-of-bounds write in the host kernel.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
case MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME_NEW:
case MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME: {
	kvmclock_reset(vcpu);

	vcpu->arch.time = data;
	kvm_make_request(KVM_REQ_CLOCK_UPDATE, vcpu);

	/* we verify if the enable bit is set... */
	if (!(data & 1))
		break;

	/* ...but clean it before doing the actual write */
	vcpu->arch.time_offset = data & ~(PAGE_MASK | 1);

	/* Check that the address is 32-byte aligned. */
	if (vcpu->arch.time_offset &
			(sizeof(struct pvclock_vcpu_time_info) - 1))
		break;

	vcpu->arch.time_page =
			gfn_to_page(vcpu->kvm, data >> PAGE_SHIFT);

	if (is_error_page(vcpu->arch.time_page))
		vcpu->arch.time_page = NULL;

	break;
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds an explicit check to ensure that the guest-provided address for `MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME` is aligned to a 32-byte boundary, which is the size of the `pvclock_vcpu_time_info` structure. If the address is not aligned, the operation is aborted, preventing the out-of-bounds write.

[Optional] Patch reference:
c300aa64ddf57d9c5d9c898a64b36877345dd4a9
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other MSR handlers in `kvm_set_msr_common` that perform memory writes based on guest-controlled addresses for similar missing alignment or bounds checks.
- Fuzz the KVM MSR handling interface with malformed MSRs to discover other potential memory corruption bugs.
- Audit the use of `gfn_to_page` and `kmap_atomic` throughout the KVM codebase to ensure all guest-controlled offsets are validated before use.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other handlers that write to guest-specified memory may have similar vulnerabilities.

