static void wdm_in_callback(struct urb *urb)
{
	struct wdm_device *desc = urb->context;
	int status = urb->status;
	int length = urb->actual_length;

	spin_lock(&desc->iuspin);
	clear_bit(WDM_RESPONDING, &desc->flags);

	if (status) {
		switch (status) {
		case -ENOENT:
			dev_dbg(&desc->intf->dev,
				"nonzero urb status received: -ENOENT");
			goto skip_error;
		case -ECONNRESET:
			dev_dbg(&desc->intf->dev,
				"nonzero urb status received: -ECONNRESET");
			goto skip_error;
		case -ESHUTDOWN:
			dev_dbg(&desc->intf->dev,
				"nonzero urb status received: -ESHUTDOWN");
			goto skip_error;
		case -EPIPE:
			dev_err(&desc->intf->dev,
				"nonzero urb status received: -EPIPE\n");
			break;
		default:
			dev_err(&desc->intf->dev,
				"Unexpected error %d\n", status);
			break;
		}
	}

	desc->rerr = status;
	if (length + desc->length > desc->wMaxCommand) {
		/* The buffer would overflow */
		set_bit(WDM_OVERFLOW, &desc->flags);
	} else {
		/* we may already be in overflow */
		if (!test_bit(WDM_OVERFLOW, &desc->flags)) {
			memmove(desc->ubuf + desc->length, desc->inbuf, length);
			desc->length += length;
			desc->reslength = length;
		}
	}
skip_error:
	wake_up(&desc->wait);

	set_bit(WDM_READ, &desc->flags);
	spin_unlock(&desc->iuspin);
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-1860

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-1860 / CWE-119:
Buffer overflow in cdc-wdm

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-03-22 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `wdm_in_callback` function within `drivers/usb/class/cdc-wdm.c`. It is triggered when a USB device provides more data than the allocated buffer can hold (`desc->wMaxCommand`). The code fails to validate the length of the incoming data before copying it with `memmove`, leading to a heap-based buffer overflow. This is exploitable because an attacker can control the size of the data transferred via a malicious USB device.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The root cause is the absence of a bounds check before copying data from `urb->actual_length` into the destination buffer.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Physical access via a malicious USB device.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious USB device connected to the system.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
static void wdm_in_callback(struct urb *urb)
{
...
	int length = urb->actual_length;
...
	desc->rerr = status;
	if (length + desc->length > desc->wMaxCommand) {
		/* The buffer would overflow */
		set_bit(WDM_OVERFLOW, &desc->flags);
	} else {
		/* we may already be in overflow */
		if (!test_bit(WDM_OVERFLOW, &desc->flags)) {
			memmove(desc->ubuf + desc->length, desc->inbuf, length);
			desc->length += length;
			desc->reslength = length;
		}
	}
...
}
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Malicious USB device.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Heap out-of-bounds write.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
Exploitation requires physical access to connect a malicious USB device but can lead to local privilege escalation.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker prepares a malicious USB device.
- Device is connected to the target system.
- The device's driver callback (`wdm_in_callback`) is invoked.
- The device sends a response with a length larger than `wMaxCommand`.
- The vulnerable code performs an out-of-bounds `memmove`, corrupting the heap.
- This corruption is leveraged to achieve local privilege escalation.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
static void wdm_in_callback(struct urb *urb)
{
...
	int length = urb->actual_length;
...
	desc->rerr = status;
	if (length + desc->length > desc->wMaxCommand) {
		/* The buffer would overflow */
		set_bit(WDM_OVERFLOW, &desc->flags);
	} else {
		/* we may already be in overflow */
		if (!test_bit(WDM_OVERFLOW, &desc->flags)) {
			memmove(desc->ubuf + desc->length, desc->inbuf, length);
			desc->length += length;
			desc->reslength = length;
		}
	}
...
}
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a conditional check to verify that the incoming data length (`length`) does not exceed the remaining space in the destination buffer (`desc->wMaxCommand - desc->length`). If an overflow would occur, it sets an overflow flag and prevents the `memmove` operation, thus avoiding the buffer overflow.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/c0f5ecee4e741667b2493c742b60b6218d40b3aa
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other USB drivers in the Linux kernel for similar patterns of unchecked `urb->actual_length` usage.
- Apply differential fuzzing to USB driver callbacks, focusing on data length validation.
- Audit the `cdc-wdm` driver for any other potential integer overflows or logic errors related to buffer management.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar unchecked buffer copy vulnerabilities may exist in other kernel USB drivers.

