    lblock += nr_blks;
    if (!buffer_new(&bh_map))
        continue;
    if (unlikely(!buffer_zeronew(&bh_map))) {
        error = -EIO;
        goto out;
    }
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
fallocate syscall

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write, kernel memory corruption

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is locally exploitable with low complexity, requiring a user to have access to a GFS2 filesystem, but it may be constrained by kernel memory protection mechanisms.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker creates or accesses a file on a GFS2 filesystem.
- Attacker calls the `fallocate` syscall with specific `len` and `offset` values to trigger the vulnerability.
- The kernel executes the vulnerable `fallocate_chunk` function.
- Improper block mapping leads to an out-of-bounds write, corrupting kernel memory.
- This corruption could be leveraged to escalate privileges or cause a denial of service.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
while (len) {
    struct buffer_head bh_map = { .b_state = 0, .b_blocknr = 0 };
    bh_map.b_size = len;
    set_buffer_zeronew(&bh_map);

    error = gfs2_block_map(inode, lblock, &bh_map, 1);
    if (unlikely(error))
        goto out;
    len -= bh_map.b_size;
    nr_blks = bh_map.b_size >> inode->i_blkbits;
    lblock += nr_blks;
    if (!buffer_new(&bh_map))
        continue;
    if (unlikely(!buffer_zeronew(&bh_map))) {
        error = -EIO;
        goto out;
    }
}
if (offset + len > inode->i_size && !(mode & FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE))
    i_size_write(inode, offset + len);
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch rewrites the fallocate logic to allocate and zero blocks directly via the block mapping layer, avoiding the problematic page cache path that caused memory corruption.

