annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-2234

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-2234 / CWE-119:
Info leak in notify messages

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: [2013-07-04] — Patch release: [Not available in input]

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
[Not available in input]

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `key_notify_sa_flush` function in `net/key/af_key.c`. When allocating and populating a `sadb_msg` structure for broadcast, the `sadb_msg_reserved` field is not initialized. This results in 2 bytes of uninitialized heap memory being leaked to listeners when the message is sent via `pfkey_broadcast`, as shown in the code excerpt where the field is left untouched.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Failure to initialize the `sadb_msg_reserved` member of the `sadb_msg` structure before broadcasting.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel PF_KEY socket listeners
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Kernel memory information leak
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
PF_KEY broadcast message
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static int key_notify_sa_flush(const struct km_event *c)
{
	struct sk_buff *skb;
	struct sadb_msg *hdr;

	skb = alloc_skb(sizeof(struct sadb_msg) + 16, GFP_ATOMIC);
	if (!skb)
		return -ENOBUFS;
	hdr = (struct sadb_msg *) skb_put(skb, sizeof(struct sadb_msg));
	hdr->sadb_msg_satype = pfkey_proto2satype(c->data.proto);
	hdr->sadb_msg_type = SADB_FLUSH;
	hdr->sadb_msg_seq = c->seq;
	hdr->sadb_msg_pid = c->portid;
	hdr->sadb_msg_version = PF_KEY_V2;
	hdr->sadb_msg_errno = (uint8_t) 0;
	hdr->sadb_msg_len = (sizeof(struct sadb_msg) / sizeof(uint64_t));

	pfkey_broadcast(skb, GFP_ATOMIC, BROADCAST_ALL, NULL, c->net);

	return 0;
}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
PF_KEY broadcast socket

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Information leak (2 bytes of uninitialized kernel memory)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is easy to trigger for any process with permissions to listen on a PF_KEY socket, allowing it to read leaked kernel memory from the broadcasted message.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Open a PF_KEY socket.
- Listen for broadcast messages.
- Trigger a SA flush event (e.g., via `ip xfrm state flush`).
- Parse the received `sadb_msg` structure and extract the `sadb_msg_reserved` field.
- The extracted value contains leaked kernel memory.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static int key_notify_sa_flush(const struct km_event *c)
{
	struct sk_buff *skb;
	struct sadb_msg *hdr;

	skb = alloc_skb(sizeof(struct sadb_msg) + 16, GFP_ATOMIC);
	if (!skb)
		return -ENOBUFS;
	hdr = (struct sadb_msg *) skb_put(skb, sizeof(struct sadb_msg));
	hdr->sadb_msg_satype = pfkey_proto2satype(c->data.proto);
	hdr->sadb_msg_type = SADB_FLUSH;
	hdr->sadb_msg_seq = c->seq;
	hdr->sadb_msg_pid = c->portid;
	hdr->sadb_msg_version = PF_KEY_V2;
	hdr->sadb_msg_errno = (uint8_t) 0;
	hdr->sadb_msg_len = (sizeof(struct sadb_msg) / sizeof(uint64_t));
	hdr->sadb_msg_reserved = 0;

	pfkey_broadcast(skb, GFP_ATOMIC, BROADCAST_ALL, NULL, c->net);

	return 0;
}
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch initializes the `sadb_msg_reserved` field of the `sadb_msg` structure to zero before broadcasting the message, preventing the information leak.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/a5cc68f3d63306d0d288f31edfc2ae6ef8ecd887
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- PatchType: Logic refactor (field initialization)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other `key_notify_*` functions in `af_key.c` for similar uninitialized fields.
- Fuzz all PF_KEY message handlers to find other potential information leaks.
- Diff forks of the Linux kernel to see if similar code paths exist without the fix.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other notification functions in the same file may leak kernel memory in a similar manner.

