[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4113

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4113 / CWE-787:
Out-of-bounds write

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-07-13 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-787
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the XML character data handler within ext/xml/xml.c, where it appends decoded character data to a string buffer without checking the parser's nesting depth. This can be triggered by processing a deeply nested XML document, causing unbounded memory allocation and a heap-based out-of-bounds write, which is exploitable due to the lack of bounds checking during string concatenation.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Unchecked parser nesting level leads to unbounded memory reallocation and string copy.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
XML parser
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of service via memory corruption; potential remote code execution.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious XML document with excessive nesting depth.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
if (parser->lastwasopen) {
    zval **myval;
    if (zend_hash_find(Z_ARRVAL_PP(parser->ctag),"value",sizeof("value"),(void **) &myval) == SUCCESS) {
        int newlen = Z_STRLEN_PP(myval) + decoded_len;
        Z_STRVAL_PP(myval) = erealloc(Z_STRVAL_PP(myval),newlen+1);
        strncpy(Z_STRVAL_PP(myval) + Z_STRLEN_PP(myval), decoded_value, decoded_len + 1);
        Z_STRLEN_PP(myval) += decoded_len;
        efree(decoded_value);
    } else {
        add_assoc_string(*(parser->ctag),"value",decoded_value,0);
    }
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Malicious XML file parsed by the affected PHP XML extension.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Heap-based out-of-bounds write due to unchecked memory allocation.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is controllable via crafted XML input, allowing an attacker to trigger memory corruption.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft an XML document with deeply nested tags to exceed the expected depth.
- Ensure the document contains character data within the nested tags to trigger the handler.
- Feed the malicious XML to a PHP script using the vulnerable XML parsing functions.
- The parser's character data handler will repeatedly reallocate a buffer without depth limits.
- This leads to an out-of-bounds write when the buffer size exceeds allocated memory.
- Resulting memory corruption can be leveraged for denial of service or further exploitation.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
if (parser->level <= XML_MAXLEVEL) {
    MAKE_STD_ZVAL(tag);
    array_init(tag);
    _xml_add_to_info(parser,parser->ltags[parser->level-1] + parser->toffset);
    add_assoc_string(tag,"tag",parser->ltags[parser->level-1] + parser->toffset,1);
    add_assoc_string(tag,"value",decoded_value,0);
    add_assoc_string(tag,"type","cdata",1);
    add_assoc_long(tag,"level",parser->level);
    zend_hash_next_index_insert(Z_ARRVAL_P(parser->data),&tag,sizeof(zval*),NULL);
} else if (parser->level == (XML_MAXLEVEL + 1)) {
    TSRMLS_FETCH();
    php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "Maximum depth exceeded - Results truncated");
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a check to ensure parser level does not exceed XML_MAXLEVEL before allocating and appending new tag data, preventing unbounded memory growth.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Not available in input
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other XML parsing functions for similar depth or size limitations.
- Fuzz the XML parser with inputs designed to trigger excessive nesting or large data payloads.
- Check for any other handlers that perform unchecked memory reallocations based on input size.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other handlers or parsers lacking depth checks may be susceptible to similar memory corruption vulnerabilities.

