files.net/ipv6/ip6_output.c:
static inline int ip6_ufo_append_data(struct sock *sk,
			int getfrag(void *from, char *to, int offset, int len,
			int odd, struct sk_buff *skb),
			void *from, int length, int hh_len, int fragheaderlen,
			int transhdrlen, int mtu,unsigned int flags,
			struct rt6_info *rt)

{
	struct sk_buff *skb;
	int err;

	/* There is support for UDP large send offload by network
	 * device, so create one single skb packet containing complete
	 * udp datagram
	 */
	if ((skb = skb_peek_tail(&sk->sk_write_queue)) == NULL) {
		struct frag_hdr fhdr;

		skb = sock_alloc_send_skb(sk,
			hh_len + fragheaderlen + transhdrlen + 20,
			(flags & MSG_DONTWAIT), &err);
		if (skb == NULL)
			return err;

		/* reserve space for Hardware header */
		skb_reserve(skb, hh_len);

		/* create space for UDP/IP header */
		skb_put(skb,fragheaderlen + transhdrlen);

		/* initialize network header pointer */
		skb_reset_network_header(skb);

		/* initialize protocol header pointer */
		skb->transport_header = skb->network_header + fragheaderlen;

		skb->protocol = htons(ETH_P_IPV6);
		skb->ip_summed = CHECKSUM_PARTIAL;
		skb->csum = 0;

		/* Specify the length of each IPv6 datagram fragment.
		 * It has to be a multiple of 8.
		 */
		skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_size = (mtu - fragheaderlen -
					     sizeof(struct frag_hdr)) & ~7;
		skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_type = SKB_GSO_UDP;
		ipv6_select_ident(&fhdr, rt);
		skb_shinfo(skb)->ip6_frag_id = fhdr.identification;
		__skb_queue_tail(&sk->sk_write_queue, skb);
	}

	return skb_append_datato_frags(sk, skb, getfrag, from,
				       (length - transhdrlen));
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4387

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4387 / CWE-119:
Memory corruption in ip6_ufo_append_data

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-10-10 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `ip6_ufo_append_data` function in `net/ipv6/ip6_output.c`. When a socket is corked, if the first UDP packet is larger than the MTU, it is processed by the UFO (UDP Fragmentation Offload) code path and a GSO (Generic Segmentation Offload) skb is created. A subsequent, smaller packet is then incorrectly appended to this GSO skb via the standard `ip6_append_data` path, leading to random memory corruption and a kernel crash.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Incorrect handling of packet enqueueing when a large packet is followed by a smaller one on a corked socket, causing the smaller packet to be appended to an incompatible GSO skb.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network stack (IPv6 UDP processing)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Kernel Denial of Service via memory corruption
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Specially crafted sequence of IPv6 UDP packets sent on a corked socket.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
static inline int ip6_ufo_append_data(struct sock *sk,
		int getfrag(void *from, char *to, int offset, int len,
		int odd, struct sk_buff *skb),
		void *from, int length, int hh_len, int fragheaderlen,
		int transhdrlen, int mtu,unsigned int flags,
		struct rt6_info *rt)
{
	// ...
	return skb_append_datato_frags(sk, skb, getfrag, from,
				       (length - transhdrlen));
}
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
IPv6 UDP network packets on a corked socket.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Kernel memory corruption leading to crash.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is reliably triggerable remotely by sending two UDP packets, where the first exceeds the path MTU and the second does not, on a corked socket, causing kernel memory corruption.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a corked IPv6 UDP socket.
- Send a first UDP datagram larger than the configured MTU.
- Send a second UDP datagram smaller than the configured MTU.
- The kernel will attempt to append the second packet to the GSO skb created for the first, corrupting memory.
- This results in a kernel panic or undefined behavior.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
static inline int ip6_ufo_append_data(struct sock *sk,
		int getfrag(void *from, char *to, int offset, int len,
		int odd, struct sk_buff *skb),
		void *from, int length, int hh_len, int fragheaderlen,
		int transhdrlen, int mtu,unsigned int flags,
		struct rt6_info *rt)
{
	struct sk_buff *skb;
	int err;

	/* There is support for UDP large send offload by network
	 * device, so create one single skb packet containing complete
	 * udp datagram
	 */
	if ((skb = skb_peek_tail(&sk->sk_write_queue)) == NULL) {
		struct frag_hdr fhdr;

		skb = sock_alloc_send_skb(sk,
			hh_len + fragheaderlen + transhdrlen + 20,
			(flags & MSG_DONTWAIT), &err);
		if (skb == NULL)
			return err;

		/* reserve space for Hardware header */
		skb_reserve(skb, hh_len);

		/* create space for UDP/IP header */
		skb_put(skb,fragheaderlen + transhdrlen);

		/* initialize network header pointer */
		skb_reset_network_header(skb);

		/* initialize protocol header pointer */
		skb->transport_header = skb->network_header + fragheaderlen;

		skb->protocol = htons(ETH_P_IPV6);
		skb->ip_summed = CHECKSUM_PARTIAL;
		skb->csum = 0;

		/* Specify the length of each IPv6 datagram fragment.
		 * It has to be a multiple of 8.
		 */
		skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_size = (mtu - fragheaderlen -
					     sizeof(struct frag_hdr)) & ~7;
		skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_type = SKB_GSO_UDP;
		ipv6_select_ident(&fhdr, rt);
		skb_shinfo(skb)->ip6_frag_id = fhdr.identification;
		__skb_queue_tail(&sk->sk_write_queue, skb);
	}

	return skb_append_datato_frags(sk, skb, getfrag, from,
				       (length - transhdrlen));
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix ensures that the GSO-specific logic, such as setting the `gso_size` and fragment ID, is only executed when a new skb is allocated. This prevents a smaller subsequent packet from being incorrectly appended to an existing GSO skb, which was the root cause of the memory corruption.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/2811ebac2521ceac84f2bdae402455baa6a7fb47
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
Logic refactor: The GSO skb initialization was moved to occur only on the first packet in a corked queue, preventing incorrect state on subsequent packets.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other protocol handlers (e.g., IPv4) for similar corked socket logic where a large packet followed by a small one might cause inconsistent state.
- Fuzz the UDP stack with sequences of packets of varying sizes on corked sockets to uncover related state management bugs.
- Diff against vendor-specific kernel forks to ensure this fix has been backported and no conflicting changes exist.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar logic errors in other parts of the networking stack could lead to comparable memory corruption vulnerabilities.

