[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4387

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4387 / CWE-119:
ipv6: udp packets following an UFO enqueued packet need also be handled by UFO

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-10-10 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer (CWE-119)

[Mandatory] Summary:
A bug in the `ip6_append_data` function in `net/ipv6/ip6_output.c` allows a smaller UDP packet to be incorrectly appended to a previously queued GSO (Generic Segmentation Offload) packet that was larger than the MTU and handled by UFO (UDP Fragmentation Offload). This leads to random memory corruption.

[Mandatory] Root cause:
Incorrect handling of packet queuing logic when a smaller packet follows a larger, UFO-enqueued packet on a corked socket.

[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network stack (IPv6 UDP handling)

[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Memory corruption → Denial of Service or potential privilege escalation

[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Sending specifically crafted UDP packets over IPv6 to trigger the queuing condition.

[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
if (length > mtu) {
    int proto = sk->sk_protocol;
    if (dontfrag && (proto == IPPROTO_UDP || proto == IPPROTO_RAW)){
        ipv6_local_rxpmtu(sk, fl6, mtu-exthdrlen);
        return -EMSGSIZE;
    }

    if (proto == IPPROTO_UDP &&
        (rt->dst.dev->features & NETIF_F_UFO)) {

        err = ip6_ufo_append_data(sk, getfrag, from, length,
                      hh_len, fragheaderlen,
                      transhdrlen, mtu, flags, rt);
        if (err)
            goto error;
        return 0;
    }
}

if ((skb = skb_peek_tail(&sk->sk_write_queue)) == NULL)
    goto alloc_new_skb;
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
IPv6 UDP socket

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Memory corruption (random write)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploitability is dependent on specific network conditions and packet sequences but can lead to kernel memory corruption.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a corked IPv6 UDP socket.
- Send a UDP packet larger than the path MTU with `dontfrag` not set, triggering UFO handling.
- Send a subsequent UDP packet smaller than the MTU.
- The second packet is incorrectly appended to the GSO packet from the first step.
- This results in memory corruption in the kernel's networking stack.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
if ((length > mtu) && dontfrag && (sk->sk_protocol == IPPROTO_UDP ||
                   sk->sk_protocol == IPPROTO_RAW)) {
    ipv6_local_rxpmtu(sk, fl6, mtu-exthdrlen);
    return -EMSGSIZE;
}

skb = skb_peek_tail(&sk->sk_write_queue);
if (((length > mtu) ||
     (skb && skb_is_gso(skb))) &&
    (sk->sk_protocol == IPPROTO_UDP) &&
    (rt->dst.dev->features & NETIF_F_UFO)) {
    err = ip6_ufo_append_data(sk, getfrag, from, length,
                  hh_len, fragheaderlen,
                  transhdrlen, mtu, flags, rt);
    if (err)
        goto error;
    return 0;
}

if (!skb)
    goto alloc_new_skb;
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch refactors the packet queuing logic to ensure that if a previous GSO packet exists, subsequent packets that would exceed the MTU are also handled by the UFO path. It also moves the `skb_peek_tail` call before the UFO check to correctly identify the state of the write queue.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit 2811ebac2521ceac84f2bdae402455baa6a7fb47 in torvalds/linux

[Optional] Patch Analysis:
- PatchType: Logic refactor
What did the fix do?
- The fix reordered and combined the conditions for handling large packets and GSO packets to prevent smaller packets from being appended to a GSO packet handled by UFO.

[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other networking stack paths that handle packet fragmentation and GSO to ensure similar logic errors do not exist.
- Fuzz the IPv6 UDP implementation with sequences of packets of varying sizes to discover related queuing issues.
- Analyze the interaction between corking, UFO, and GSO features for other protocols (e.g., TCP).

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar queuing logic bugs could exist in other parts of the network stack.

