annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4511

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4511 / CWE-119:
Incorrectly checked remap_pfn_range calls

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: [2013-11-12] — Patch release: [2013-10-28]

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Linux kernel (pre-commit 7314e613d5ff9f0934f7a0f74ed7973b903315d1)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `au1100fb_fb_mmap` function in `drivers/video/au1100fb.c`. The original implementation performed manual validation of the VMA offset and length before calling `io_remap_pfn_range`. A subsequent change replaced this logic with a direct call to `vm_iomap_memory`, but this removed the critical bounds check on `vma->vm_pgoff`, potentially allowing a user to map memory beyond the intended framebuffer region. This is exploitable because the function lacks the validation to ensure the requested mapping size and offset are within the physical framebuffer's limits (`fbdev->fb_len`).
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Removal of bounds checks on the VMA offset and length when refactoring the memory mapping logic.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel driver (framebuffer device)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Driver IOCTL via mmap on the framebuffer device
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
int au1100fb_fb_mmap(struct fb_info *fbi, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
{
	struct au1100fb_device *fbdev;

	fbdev = to_au1100fb_device(fbi);

	vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_noncached(vma->vm_page_prot);
	pgprot_val(vma->vm_page_prot) |= (6 << 9); //CCA=6

	return vm_iomap_memory(vma, fbdev->fb_phys, fbdev->fb_len);
}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
mmap system call on the framebuffer device file

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary kernel memory mapping (read/write)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is straightforward for a local user who can access the framebuffer device, as it involves passing a crafted offset to mmap, bypassing the removed validation.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Open the framebuffer device (e.g., `/dev/fb0`).
- Calculate a large `vm_pgoff` value to target memory outside the framebuffer.
- Call `mmap` on the device file descriptor with `MAP_SHARED`, providing the calculated offset.
- The kernel maps the unintended physical memory into the process's address space.
- Read from or write to the mapped region to access or corrupt privileged kernel data.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
int au1100fb_fb_mmap(struct fb_info *fbi, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
{
	struct au1100fb_device *fbdev;
	unsigned int len;
	unsigned long start=0, off;

	fbdev = to_au1100fb_device(fbi);

	if (vma->vm_pgoff > (~0UL >> PAGE_SHIFT)) {
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	start = fbdev->fb_phys & PAGE_MASK;
	len = PAGE_ALIGN((start & ~PAGE_MASK) + fbdev->fb_len);

	off = vma->vm_pgoff << PAGE_SHIFT;

	if ((vma->vm_end - vma->vm_start + off) > len) {
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	off += start;
	vma->vm_pgoff = off >> PAGE_SHIFT;

	vma->vm_page_prot = pgprot_noncached(vma->vm_page_prot);
	pgprot_val(vma->vm_page_prot) |= (6 << 9); //CCA=6

	if (io_remap_pfn_range(vma, vma->vm_start, off >> PAGE_SHIFT,
					vma->vm_end - vma->vm_start,
					vma->vm_page_prot)) {
		return -EAGAIN;
	}

	return 0;
}
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch restored the previous implementation, which includes explicit checks on `vma->vm_pgoff` and the total requested mapping size to ensure they are within the bounds of the framebuffer's physical memory before proceeding with the mapping.

[Optional] Patch reference:
commit 7314e613d5ff9f0934f7a0f74ed7973b903315d1
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
The fix reverted a logic refactor and re-added the previously existing bounds checks to prevent out-of-bounds memory mappings.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review all instances where `vm_iomap_memory` was introduced as a replacement for manual `[io_]remap_pfn_range` calls to ensure bounds checks were not inadvertently removed.
- Fuzz the `mmap` handlers for other framebuffer and GPU drivers with crafted offsets and sizes.
- Perform a code audit of other drivers in the Linux kernel that perform memory mapping to identify similar patterns of insufficient validation.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other drivers refactored to use `vm_iomap_memory` without proper bounds checks may have similar local privilege escalation vulnerabilities.

