[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4588

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4588 / CWE-119:
Buffer overflow in IPVS get_ctl

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-11-20 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
A buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the `do_ip_vs_get_ctl` function in `net/netfilter/ipvs/ip_vs_ctl.c`. The function copies data from userspace into a fixed-size stack buffer `arg[128]` without validating that the requested size `get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]` is within the buffer's bounds. This allows a user with `CAP_NET_ADMIN` privileges to trigger an out-of-bounds write by issuing a specific ioctl with a large length parameter, potentially leading to kernel memory corruption.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The length of the data to be copied from userspace, `get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]`, is not checked against the destination buffer size of 128 bytes before being used in `copy_from_user`.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel driver (IPVS netfilter module)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
IOCTL call via `do_ip_vs_get_ctl`
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static int
do_ip_vs_get_ctl(struct sock *sk, int cmd, void __user *user, int *len)
{
	unsigned char arg[128];
	int ret = 0;
	unsigned int copylen;

	if (!capable(CAP_NET_ADMIN))
		return -EPERM;

	if (cmd < IP_VS_BASE_CTL || cmd > IP_VS_SO_GET_MAX)
		return -EINVAL;

	if (*len < get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]) {
		pr_err("get_ctl: len %u < %u\n",
		       *len, get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]);
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	if (copy_from_user(arg, user, get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]) != 0)
		return -EFAULT;

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
IOCTL call via `do_ip_vs_get_ctl`

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Stack-based out-of-bounds write

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
A local user with `CAP_NET_ADMIN` privileges can reliably trigger the overflow by crafting an ioctl request, potentially leading to arbitrary code execution in kernel context.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker requires `CAP_NET_ADMIN` privileges.
- Craft an `IP_VS_SO_GET_*` ioctl request.
- Set the `*len` argument to a value greater than or equal to the expected size but less than the maximum allowed by the check.
- The `get_arglen` table must contain an entry for the chosen command that is greater than 128.
- Invoke the ioctl, causing `copy_from_user` to write beyond the `arg[128]` buffer on the stack.
- This corrupts kernel memory, potentially leading to privilege escalation.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static int
do_ip_vs_get_ctl(struct sock *sk, int cmd, void __user *user, int *len)
{
	unsigned char arg[128];
	int ret = 0;
	unsigned int copylen;

	if (!capable(CAP_NET_ADMIN))
		return -EPERM;

	if (cmd < IP_VS_BASE_CTL || cmd > IP_VS_SO_GET_MAX)
		return -EINVAL;

	if (*len < get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]) {
		pr_err("get_ctl: len %u < %u\n",
		       *len, get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)]);
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	copylen = get_arglen[GET_CMDID(cmd)];
	if (copylen > 128)
		return -EINVAL;

	if (copy_from_user(arg, user, copylen) != 0)
		return -EFAULT;

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a bounds check to ensure `copylen`, derived from the `get_arglen` table, does not exceed the size of the destination stack buffer `arg[128]`. This prevents the buffer overflow by returning an error for any command requesting a copy size larger than the buffer.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/04bcef2a83f40c6db24222b27a52892cba39dffb
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the IPVS codebase that use `copy_from_user` with fixed-size buffers for similar missing bounds checks.
- Audit the `get_arglen` table to ensure all entries are sane and correspond to valid, bounded operations.
- Fuzz the IPVS ioctl handlers with oversized length parameters to discover any remaining overflow conditions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other ioctl handlers in the kernel with similar patterns may be vulnerable to the same class of buffer overflow.

