[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4588

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4588 / CWE-119:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-11-20 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `do_ip_vs_set_ctl` function within `net/netfilter/ipvs/ip_vs_ctl.c`. The function processes user-supplied data via an ioctl but fails to properly validate the `cmd` and `len` parameters before copying data from userspace. This allows a local attacker to supply a length value outside of the intended bounds, leading to a buffer overflow when `copy_from_user` is called.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The root cause is the lack of boundary checks on the `cmd` and `len` parameters before using `len` to copy data from userspace into a fixed-size stack buffer.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Local system, requiring CAP_NET_ADMIN capability.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious ioctl call with an out-of-bounds `len` argument.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static int
do_ip_vs_set_ctl(struct sock *sk, int cmd, void __user *user, unsigned int len)
{
	int ret;
	unsigned char arg[MAX_ARG_LEN];
	struct ip_vs_service_user *usvc_compat;
	struct ip_vs_service_user_kern usvc;
	struct ip_vs_service *svc;
	struct ip_vs_dest_user *udest_compat;
	struct ip_vs_dest_user_kern udest;

	if (!capable(CAP_NET_ADMIN))
		return -EPERM;

	if (len != set_arglen[SET_CMDID(cmd)]) {
		pr_err("set_ctl: len %u != %u\n",
		       len, set_arglen[SET_CMDID(cmd)]);
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	if (copy_from_user(arg, user, len) != 0)
		return -EFAULT;

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
ioctl on the IPVS netfilter module.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Stack-based buffer overflow (out-of-bounds write).

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploitability is high for a local attacker with the CAP_NET_ADMIN capability, as it allows controlled corruption of kernel stack memory.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker requires CAP_NET_ADMIN privilege.
- Craft an ioctl call with a `cmd` value within the IP_VS_SO_SET range.
- Set the `len` parameter to a value larger than `MAX_ARG_LEN`.
- The `len` check against `set_arglen` can be bypassed for certain commands.
- Call `ioctl`, triggering `copy_from_user` to write past the `arg` buffer's boundary.
- Resulting overflow corrupts the stack, potentially leading to privilege escalation.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
	if (!capable(CAP_NET_ADMIN))
		return -EPERM;

	if (cmd < IP_VS_BASE_CTL || cmd > IP_VS_SO_SET_MAX)
		return -EINVAL;
	if (len < 0 || len >  MAX_ARG_LEN)
		return -EINVAL;
	if (len != set_arglen[SET_CMDID(cmd)]) {
		pr_err("set_ctl: len %u != %u\n",
		       len, set_arglen[SET_CMDID(cmd)]);
		return -EINVAL;
	}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds explicit boundary checks for the `cmd` and `len` ioctl arguments before any data is copied from userspace, preventing the buffer overflow.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Not available in input
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- PatchType: bounds check added
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other netfilter ioctl handlers for similar missing argument validation.
- Fuzz the IPVS ioctl interface with out-of-range command and length values.
- Audit other kernel subsystems that use `copy_from_user` with user-controlled length arguments.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other ioctl handlers in the kernel may have similar missing bounds checks.

