[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-4473

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-4473 / CWE-119:
Buffer overflow in pathName

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-11-23 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
poppler (pre-commit b8682d868ddf7f741e93b)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `extractPages` function in `utils/pdfseparate.cc`. It is triggered when processing a specially crafted `destFileName` string that, when formatted with a page number, results in a path longer than the fixed-size `pathName` buffer. The unsafe use of `sprintf` allows for a buffer overflow, which can lead to memory corruption and is exploitable.

[Mandatory] Root cause:
Use of the unsafe `sprintf` function without bounds checking on the destination buffer `pathName`.

[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Local file processing utility (pdfseparate)

[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation / Arbitrary code execution

[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A malicious `destFileName` argument supplied to the pdfseparate utility.

[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
  char pathName[4096];
  // ...
  for (int pageNo = firstPage; pageNo <= lastPage; pageNo++) {
    snprintf (pathName, sizeof (pathName) - 1, destFileName, pageNo);
    GooString *gpageName = new GooString (pathName);
    int errCode = doc->savePageAs(gpageName, pageNo);
    if ( errCode != errNone) {
      delete gpageName;
      delete gfileName;
      return false;
    }
    delete gpageName;
  }
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Command-line argument `destFileName` to the pdfseparate utility.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Stack-based buffer overflow (write beyond bounds).

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is easily exploitable by a local user providing a long format string as an argument, potentially leading to code execution.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker constructs a long file path format string (e.g., `AAAA...%d`).
- Attacker invokes pdfseparate with this string as the destination filename argument.
- The `sprintf` call in the vulnerable version copies the formatted string into the 1024-byte `pathName` buffer, overflowing it.
- The overflow overwrites adjacent stack data, including the return address.
- Controlled execution is achieved when the function returns.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
  char pathName[4096];
  // ...
  for (int pageNo = firstPage; pageNo <= lastPage; pageNo++) {
    snprintf (pathName, sizeof (pathName) - 1, destFileName, pageNo);
    GooString *gpageName = new GooString (pathName);
    int errCode = doc->savePageAs(gpageName, pageNo);
    if ( errCode != errNone) {
      delete gpageName;
      delete gfileName;
      return false;
    }
    delete gpageName;
  }
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The unsafe `sprintf` was replaced with `snprintf` to limit the number of bytes written to the size of the `pathName` buffer, and the buffer size was increased from 1024 to 4096 bytes.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit b8682d868ddf7f741e93b791588af0932893f95c

[Optional] Patch Analysis:
- PatchType: Bounds check added / API change
What did the fix do?: Replaced `sprintf` with `snprintf` to prevent buffer overflow and increased the buffer size to mitigate issues with very long paths.

[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other uses of `sprintf` within the `utils` directory for similar buffer overflow risks.
- Fuzz the pdfseparate utility with various long and malformed `destFileName` inputs to ensure no other overflows exist.
- Audit other command-line utilities in the project that perform similar string formatting into fixed-size buffers.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other utilities in the project may contain similar `sprintf`-based buffer overflows.

