[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-6381

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-6381 / CWE-119:
Buffer overflow in snmp ioctl

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: [2013-11-27] — Patch release: [Not available in input]

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
[Not available in input]

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
 The bug is in the `qeth_snmp_command` function in `drivers/s390/net/qeth_core_main.c`. It is triggered when a user-provided length (`req_len`) from an IOCTL request is used to allocate and copy data without sufficient bounds checking. This can lead to a kernel buffer overflow because the length is not validated against the size of the destination command buffer before being used in `memdup_user` and `memcpy`, making it exploitable.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 Unchecked user-provided length field used for memory allocation and copy operations.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel driver (qeth network driver)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
 IOCTL call
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

	ureq = memdup_user(udata, req_len + sizeof(struct qeth_snmp_ureq_hdr));
	if (IS_ERR(ureq)) {
		QETH_CARD_TEXT(card, 2, "snmpnome");
		return PTR_ERR(ureq);
	}
	qinfo.udata_len = ureq->hdr.data_len;
	qinfo.udata = kzalloc(qinfo.udata_len, GFP_KERNEL);
	if (!qinfo.udata) {
