annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2013-6712

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-6712 / CWE-119:
Heap buffer over-read in DateInterval

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-11-28 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `scan` function within `ext/date/lib/parse_iso_intervals.c`, where a loop parsing period specifiers continues until `*ptr` is null without checking for an error condition. This can be triggered by a malformed ISO 8601 interval string, causing the parser to read past the intended buffer. The vulnerability is exploitable because the loop's termination condition does not account for error states set during parsing, leading to an out-of-bounds read.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The loop's termination condition `while (*ptr)` lacks a check for the parser's error count, allowing continuation even after an error is flagged.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Date parsing library (timelib)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service (via crash) or potential information disclosure
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malformed ISO 8601 interval string provided to a date parsing function.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
		do {
			if ( *ptr == 'T' ) {
				in_time = 1;
				ptr++;
			}
			if ( *ptr == '\0' ) {
				add_error(s, "Missing expected time part");
				break;
			}

			nr = timelib_get_unsigned_nr((char **) &ptr, 12);
			switch (*ptr) {
				case 'Y': s->period->y = nr; break;
				case 'W': s->period->d = nr * 7; break;
				case 'D': s->period->d = nr; break;
				case 'H': s->period->h = nr; break;
				case 'S': s->period->s = nr; break;
				case 'M': 
					if (in_time) {
						s->period->i = nr;
					} else {
						s->period->m = nr; 
					}
					break;
				default:
					add_error(s, "Undefined period specifier");
					break;
			}
			ptr++;
		} while (*ptr);
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Date parsing API
[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read
[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is easy to trigger with a crafted string but is limited by the context of the parsing function.
[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft an ISO 8601 interval string that causes an error during parsing (e.g., undefined specifier).
- Ensure the error occurs before the end of the string.
- The loop will continue reading past the buffer's logical end due to the missing error check.
- This can lead to a crash or leak adjacent memory contents.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
		do {
			if ( *ptr == 'T' ) {
				in_time = 1;
				ptr++;
			}
			if ( *ptr == '\0' ) {
				add_error(s, "Missing expected time part");
				break;
			}

			nr = timelib_get_unsigned_nr((char **) &ptr, 12);
			switch (*ptr) {
				case 'Y': s->period->y = nr; break;
				case 'W': s->period->d = nr * 7; break;
				case 'D': s->period->d = nr; break;
				case 'H': s->period->h = nr; break;
				case 'S': s->period->s = nr; break;
				case 'M': 
					if (in_time) {
						s->period->i = nr;
					} else {
						s->period->m = nr; 
					}
					break;
				default:
					add_error(s, "Undefined period specifier");
					break;
			}
			ptr++;
		} while (!s->errors->error_count && *ptr);
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch modified the loop's termination condition to `while (!s->errors->error_count && *ptr);`, ensuring the loop exits if an error is set during parsing.
[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit a0bb3fd6793fe16dbf4d3b5eb3413093088a6b37
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- PatchType: logic refactor
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other parsing loops within `timelib` for similar missing error-count checks.
- Fuzz the date parsing library with malformed input to identify related issues.
- Audit other `do-while` loops that depend on external state for termination.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar over-read vulnerabilities may exist in other date parsing components.

