static time_t asn1_time_to_time_t(ASN1_UTCTIME * timestr TSRMLS_DC) /* {{{ */
{
/*
	This is how the time string is formatted:

   snprintf(p, sizeof(p), "%02d%02d%02d%02d%02d%02dZ",ts->tm_year%100,
      ts->tm_mon+1,ts->tm_mday,ts->tm_hour,ts->tm_min,ts->tm_sec);
*/

	time_t ret;
	struct tm thetime;
	char * strbuf;
	char * thestr;
	long gmadjust = 0;

	if (ASN1_STRING_type(timestr) != V_ASN1_UTCTIME) {
		php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "illegal ASN1 data type for timestamp");
		return (time_t)-1;
	}

	if (ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) != strlen(ASN1_STRING_data(timestr))) {
		php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "illegal length in timestamp");
		return (time_t)-1;
	}

	if (ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) < 13) {
		php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "unable to parse time string %s correctly", timestr->data);
		return (time_t)-1;
	}

	strbuf = estrdup((char *)ASN1_STRING_data(timestr));

	memset(&thetime, 0, sizeof(thetime));

	/* we work backwards so that we can use atoi more easily */

	thestr = strbuf + ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) - 3;

	thetime.tm_sec = atoi(thestr);
	*thestr = '\0';
	thestr -= 2;
	thetime.tm_min = atoi(thestr);
	*thestr = '\0';
	thestr -= 2;
	thetime.tm_hour = atoi(thestr);
	*thestr = '\0';
	thestr -= 2;
	thetime.tm_mday = atoi(thestr);
	*thestr = '\0';
	thestr -= 2;
	thetime.tm_mon = atoi(thestr)-1;
	*thestr = '\0';
	thestr -= 2;
	thetime.tm_year = atoi(thestr);

	if (thetime.tm_year < 68) {
		thetime.tm_year += 100;
	}

	thetime.tm_isdst = -1;
	ret = mktime(&thetime);

#if HAVE_TM_GMTOFF
	gmadjust = thetime.tm_gmtoff;
#else
	/*
	** If correcting for daylight savings time, we set the adjustment to
	** the value of timezone - 3600 seconds. Otherwise, we need to overcorrect and
	** set the adjustment to the main timezone + 3600 seconds.
	*/
	gmadjust = -(thetime.tm_isdst ? (long)timezone - 3600 : (long)timezone + 3600);
#endif
	ret += gmadjust;

	efree(strbuf);

	return ret;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID: CVE-2013-6420

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2013-6420 / CWE-119:
Memory corruption in asn1_time_to_time_t

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2013-12-17 — Patch release: 2013-12-17

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `asn1_time_to_time_t` function within `ext/openssl/openssl.c`. The function parses an ASN1_UTCTIME string by duplicating it into a buffer and then walking backwards, writing null terminators to separate date components. The original code failed to validate the length of the input string before performing pointer arithmetic (`thestr = strbuf + timestr->length - 3`), which could result in a buffer underflow and memory corruption if the string was shorter than expected. This is exploitable because an attacker can control the `timestr` input, leading to an out-of-bounds write.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The pointer arithmetic to calculate the start of the seconds field (`thestr = strbuf + timestr->length - 3`) was performed without sufficient validation of the input string's length.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network service (via OpenSSL X.509 certificate parsing)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Memory corruption leading to potential Denial of Service or Remote Code Execution.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A crafted X.509 certificate containing a malformed ASN1_UTCTIME field.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

```c
if (ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) < 13) {
    php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "unable to parse time string %s correctly", timestr->data);
    return (time_t)-1;
}

strbuf = estrdup((char *)ASN1_STRING_data(timestr));

memset(&thetime, 0, sizeof(thetime));

/* we work backwards so that we can use atoi more easily */

thestr = strbuf + ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) - 3;
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
X.509 certificate parsing via OpenSSL extension.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write (buffer underflow).

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is triggered by parsing a specially crafted certificate, allowing an attacker to corrupt memory with controlled data.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create an X.509 certificate with a malformed `validity.notAfter` or `validity.notBefore` field.
- The ASN1_UTCTIME string in this field should be shorter than 13 characters.
- Supply this certificate to an application using the vulnerable PHP `openssl_x509_parse` function.
- The `asn1_time_to_time_t` function will process the short string.
- The pointer arithmetic `strbuf + length - 3` will underflow, writing a null byte outside the allocated buffer.
- This results in memory corruption.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
if (ASN1_STRING_type(timestr) != V_ASN1_UTCTIME) {
    php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "illegal ASN1 data type for timestamp");
    return (time_t)-1;
}

if (ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) != strlen(ASN1_STRING_data(timestr))) {
    php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "illegal length in timestamp");
    return (time_t)-1;
}

if (ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) < 13) {
    php_error_docref(NULL TSRMLS_CC, E_WARNING, "unable to parse time string %s correctly", timestr->data);
    return (time_t)-1;
}

strbuf = estrdup((char *)ASN1_STRING_data(timestr));

memset(&thetime, 0, sizeof(thetime));

/* we work backwards so that we can use atoi more easily */

thestr = strbuf + ASN1_STRING_length(timestr) - 3;
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds two new validation checks before the vulnerable pointer arithmetic. It first verifies that the ASN1 string type is `V_ASN1_UTCTIME` and then ensures that the string's reported length matches its actual data length, in addition to the existing check for a minimum length of 13. This prevents the underflow condition.

[Optional] Patch reference:
commit c1224573c773b6845e83505f717fbf820fc18415
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- PatchType: Bounds check added.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other ASN1 string parsing functions within the OpenSSL extension for similar length validation issues.
- Fuzz the `openssl_x509_parse` function and other certificate parsing functions with malformed ASN1 structures.
- Audit other extensions in the PHP codebase that parse ASN1 data for similar patterns of pointer arithmetic without robust length checks.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other functions parsing ASN1_TIME or similar structures may have comparable buffer underflow vulnerabilities.

