static void *proc_pid_follow_link(struct dentry *dentry, struct nameidata *nd)
{
	struct inode *inode = dentry->d_inode;
	int error = -EACCES;

	/* We don't need a base pointer in the /proc filesystem */
	path_put(&nd->path);

	/* Are we allowed to snoop on the tasks file descriptors? */
	if (!proc_fd_access_allowed(inode))
		goto out;

	error = PROC_I(inode)->op.proc_get_link(inode, &nd->path);
out:
	return ERR_PTR(error);
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID: CVE-2014-0203

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2014-0203 / CWE-416: Use After Free in proc_pid_follow_link

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2014-06-23 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-416: Use After Free
[Mandatory] Summary:
A use-after-free vulnerability exists in the `proc_pid_follow_link` function within `fs/proc/base.c`. After calling `path_put(&nd->path)`, the function improperly sets `nd->last_type = LAST_BIND`. This operation writes to memory that may have been freed by `path_put`, leading to a use-after-free condition that can be triggered by a local user accessing specific proc filesystem entries.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The function writes to the `nd->last_type` field after the memory for `nd->path` may have been released by a preceding `path_put` call.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Local filesystem access via the proc filesystem.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Accessing a symbolic link within the /proc filesystem, potentially involving autofs or other special mountpoints.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static void *proc_pid_follow_link(struct dentry *dentry, struct nameidata *nd)
{
	struct inode *inode = dentry->d_inode;
	int error = -EACCES;

	/* We don't need a base pointer in the /proc filesystem */
	path_put(&nd->path);

	/* Are we allowed to snoop on the tasks file descriptors? */
	if (!proc_fd_access_allowed(inode))
		goto out;

	error = PROC_I(inode)->op.proc_get_link(inode, &nd->path);
	nd->last_type = LAST_BIND;
out:
	return ERR_PTR(error);
}

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Filesystem operations on /proc entries, specifically following symlinks.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Use-after-free write, potentially leading to arbitrary code execution or privilege escalation.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is triggered locally and requires a specific sequence of file operations, but provides a strong primitive for escalating privileges due to the kernel-space use-after-free.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Not available in input

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static void *proc_pid_follow_link(struct dentry *dentry, struct nameidata *nd)
{
	struct inode *inode = dentry->d_inode;
	int error = -EACCES;

	/* We don't need a base pointer in the /proc filesystem */
	path_put(&nd->path);

	/* Are we allowed to snoop on the tasks file descriptors? */
	if (!proc_fd_access_allowed(inode))
		goto out;

	error = PROC_I(inode)->op.proc_get_link(inode, &nd->path);
out:
	return ERR_PTR(error);
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch removes the line `nd->last_type = LAST_BIND;` to prevent writing to the `nd` structure after `path_put()` may have freed its associated memory, thus resolving the use-after-free vulnerability.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/86acdca1b63e6890540fa19495cfc708beff3d8b
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- PatchType: Logic refactor (removal of unsafe write)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the kernel that call `path_put()` and subsequently access the `nameidata` structure.
- Fuzz filesystem interfaces, especially those involving symlinks and special mountpoints like autofs, to uncover similar use-after-free patterns.
- Audit changes to `nameidata` handling across different kernel versions for regressions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other instances of post-`path_put` access to the `nameidata` structure could lead to similar use-after-free vulnerabilities.

