[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2014-0207

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2014-0207 / CWE-119:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2014-07-09 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `cdf_file_property_info` function within `src/readcdf.c`. When processing CDF_LENGTH32_STRING or CDF_LENGTH32_WSTRING property types, the code copies string data from a source buffer into a fixed-size stack buffer `vbuf[1024]`. The loop condition `for (j = 0; j < sizeof(vbuf) && len--; j++, s += k)` ensures the destination buffer `vbuf` is not overflowed. However, the source pointer `s` is advanced without a corresponding check that the remaining source length is valid, potentially leading to an out-of-bounds read on the source data if the provided length `len` is larger than the actual source buffer size. This can be triggered by a crafted file with a malicious string length field.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The loop that copies string data trusts the length field from the input file and does not validate it against the actual size of the source buffer before performing reads.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
File parser (file utility)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious CDF file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
                case CDF_LENGTH32_STRING:
                case CDF_LENGTH32_WSTRING:
                        len = info[i].pi_str.s_len;
                        if (len > 1) {
                                char vbuf[1024];
                                size_t j, k = 1;

                                if (info[i].pi_type == CDF_LENGTH32_WSTRING)
                                    k++;
                                s = info[i].pi_str.s_buf;
                                for (j = 0; j < sizeof(vbuf) && len--;
                                    j++, s += k) {
                                        if (*s == '\0')
                                                break;
                                        if (isprint((unsigned char)*s))
                                                vbuf[j] = *s;
                                }
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File parsing via the 'file' utility on a crafted CDF document.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger an out-of-bounds read crash by providing a CDF file with a string property whose length field exceeds the size of its associated data buffer.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a CDF file containing a property of type CDF_LENGTH32_STRING or CDF_LENGTH32_WSTRING.
- Set the string length field (`pi_str.s_len`) to a value larger than the actual data buffer.
- Ensure the buffer contains insufficient data to satisfy the length.
- Run the `file` utility on the crafted file.
- The utility will read past the end of the source buffer, potentially causing a segmentation fault.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
                case CDF_LENGTH32_STRING:
                case CDF_LENGTH32_WSTRING:
                        len = info[i].pi_str.s_len;
                        if (len > 1) {
                                char vbuf[1024];
                                size_t j, k = 1;

                                if (info[i].pi_type == CDF_LENGTH32_WSTRING)
                                    k++;
                                s = info[i].pi_str.s_buf;
                                for (j = 0; j < sizeof(vbuf) && len-- && *s != '\0';
                                    j++, s += k) {
                                        if (isprint((unsigned char)*s))
                                                vbuf[j] = *s;
                                }
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch modifies the loop condition to ensure that the loop also terminates if the source character `*s` is a null terminator, preventing advancing the pointer `s` past the actual end of the source string data.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Not available in input
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other string parsing routines within the CDF parser for similar reliance on untrusted length fields.
- Fuzz the CDF parser with a focus on malformed property structures and length fields.
- Audit all loops that decrement a length counter while advancing a data pointer to ensure they have adequate termination conditions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds read issues may exist in other parts of the CDF parser that handle variable-length data.

