annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2014-3478

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2014-3478 / CWE-119:
Incorrect handling of truncated pascal string size

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2014-07-09 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `mconvert` function within `ext/fileinfo/libmagic/softmagic.c` when processing Pascal strings (FILE_PSTRING). The function calculates the string length (`len`) from the input but fails to correctly account for the size of the length prefix (`sz`) when truncating the string to fit the destination buffer `p->s`. This can result in an out-of-bounds write during the `while (len--) *ptr1++ = *ptr2++;` loop, as `ptr2` may read beyond the intended source data and `ptr1` may write past the end of `p->s` before the final null terminator is written.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Incorrect calculation of the maximum copy length for a Pascal string, where the size of the length prefix is not subtracted from the destination buffer size.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
File parser (libmagic)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of service via buffer overflow
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Specially crafted file processed by the fileinfo extension
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
case FILE_PSTRING: {
    size_t sz = file_pstring_length_size(m);
    char *ptr1 = p->s, *ptr2 = ptr1 + sz;
    size_t len = file_pstring_get_length(m, ptr1);
    if (len >= sizeof(p->s)) {
        len = sizeof(p->s) - sz;
    }
    while (len--)
        *ptr1++ = *ptr2++;
    *ptr1 = '\0';
    return 1;
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Specially crafted file parsed by the fileinfo library

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Stack-based buffer overflow (out-of-bounds write)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is easily triggered by processing a malicious file containing a Pascal string with a length field that causes a buffer overflow, potentially leading to a crash.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Not available in input

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
case FILE_PSTRING: {
    size_t sz = file_pstring_length_size(m);
    char *ptr1 = p->s, *ptr2 = ptr1 + sz;
    size_t len = file_pstring_get_length(m, ptr1);
    if (len >= sizeof(p->s)) {
        /*
         * The size of the pascal string length (sz)
         * is 1, 2, or 4. We need at least 1 byte for NUL
         * termination, but we've already truncated the
         * string by p->s, so we need to deduct sz.
         */ 
        len = sizeof(p->s) - sz;
    }
    while (len--)
        *ptr1++ = *ptr2++;
    *ptr1 = '\0';
    return 1;
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix ensures that when a Pascal string's length exceeds the destination buffer, the length to be copied (`len`) is correctly reduced by the size of the length prefix (`sz`), preventing an out-of-bounds write.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/php/php-src/commit/e77659a8c87272e5061738a31430d2111482c426
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other string handling functions within `softmagic.c` for similar incorrect buffer size calculations.
- Fuzz the fileinfo parser with various file types and malformed string structures to identify related issues.
- Diff forks of the `file` project to check if this fix has been applied elsewhere.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar buffer overflows may exist in other conversion cases within the same function.

