[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2023-4751

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2023-4751 / CWE-122:
Heap-based buffer overflow

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2023-09-03 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
vim (pre-commit e1121b139480f53d1b06f84f3e4574048108fa0b)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-122: Heap-based Buffer Overflow
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `ex_buffer_all` function in `src/buffer.c`. When the command `:ball` is executed while in Visual mode, the function proceeds to manipulate buffers and windows without first resetting the Visual mode state. This can lead to an illegal memory access because the cursor and "VIsual" mark may become invalid after switching to another buffer, as shown by the loop that iterates through buffers and opens windows (`for (buf = firstbuf; ...)`).
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Failure to stop Visual mode before performing buffer and window operations.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Text editor command-line interface
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local application crash or potential code execution
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Executing the `:ball` command while in Visual mode.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
void
ex_buffer_all(exarg_T *eap)
{
    buf_T	*buf;
    win_T	*wp, *wpnext;
    int		split_ret = OK;
    int		p_ea_save;
    int		open_wins = 0;
    int		r;
    int		count;		// Maximum number of windows to open.
    int		all;		// When TRUE also load inactive buffers.
    int		had_tab = cmdmod.cmod_tab;
    tabpage_T	*tpnext;

    if (eap->addr_count == 0)	// make as many windows as possible
	count = 9999;
    else
	count = eap->line2;	// make as many windows as specified
    if (eap->cmdidx == CMD_unhide || eap->cmdidx == CMD_sunhide)
	all = FALSE;
    else
	all = TRUE;

    setpcmark();
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Vim command-line (`:ball`)

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Illegal memory access, potential out-of-bounds read/write

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is triggered locally by a user with access to the Vim editor, requiring a specific sequence of commands to enter Visual mode and then execute `:ball`.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Open a file in Vim.
- Enter Visual mode (e.g., by pressing `v`).
- Execute the `:ball` command.
- The application will access invalid memory, leading to a crash.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
void
ex_buffer_all(exarg_T *eap)
{
    buf_T	*buf;
    win_T	*wp, *wpnext;
    int		split_ret = OK;
    int		p_ea_save;
    int		open_wins = 0;
    int		r;
    int		count;		// Maximum number of windows to open.
    int		all;		// When TRUE also load inactive buffers.
    int		had_tab = cmdmod.cmod_tab;
    tabpage_T	*tpnext;

    if (eap->addr_count == 0)	// make as many windows as possible
	count = 9999;
    else
	count = eap->line2;	// make as many windows as specified
    if (eap->cmdidx == CMD_unhide || eap->cmdidx == CMD_sunhide)
	all = FALSE;
    else
	all = TRUE;

    // Stop Visual mode, the cursor and "VIsual" may very well be invalid after
    // switching to another buffer.
    reset_VIsual_and_resel();

    setpcmark();

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a call to `reset_VIsual_and_resel()` at the beginning of the `ex_buffer_all` function to ensure Visual mode is exited before any buffer or window manipulation occurs.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit e1121b139480f53d1b06f84f3e4574048108fa0b
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions that manipulate buffers or windows to ensure they also reset Visual mode.
- Fuzz Vim commands that can be executed from Visual mode to identify similar issues.
- Check for other entry points where buffer switching occurs without proper state cleanup.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other commands that switch buffers or windows from Visual mode may be susceptible to similar memory corruption issues.

