[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2023-36271

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2023-36271 / CWE-787:
Out-of-bounds write in UTF-8 conversion

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2023-06-23 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
LibreDWG/libredwg (pre-commit c1ed1d91e28a6ddc7a9b5479d4795d58fb6be0ca)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `bit_utf8_to_TU` function within `src/bits.c`, which converts a UTF-8 string to a wide-character string. It is triggered when processing a specially crafted invalid UTF-8 input string that causes the function to miscalculate the length of remaining bytes. This leads to reading past the input buffer and writing beyond the bounds of the allocated output buffer (`wstr`), making it exploitable for memory corruption.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The function fails to properly validate multi-byte UTF-8 sequences before accessing subsequent bytes, leading to out-of-bounds reads and writes when processing malformed input.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
File parser (e.g., DWG file processing), library function
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Out-of-bounds write → Potential Remote Code Execution
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malformed UTF-8 string in a processed file (e.g., a crafted DWG file)
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
      else if ((c & 0xf0) == 0xe0)
        {
          /* ignore invalid utf8? */
          if (len >= 2
              && ((unsigned char)str[1] < 0x80 || (unsigned char)str[1] > 0xBF
                  || (unsigned char)str[2] < 0x80
                  || (unsigned char)str[2] > 0xBF))
            {
              LOG_WARN ("utf-8: BAD_CONTINUATION_BYTE %s", str);
            }
          else if (len >= 1 && c == 0xe0 && (unsigned char)str[1] < 0xa0)
            {
              LOG_WARN ("utf-8: NON_SHORTEST %s", str);
            }
          if (len >= 2)
            wstr[i++] = ((c & 0x0f) << 12) | ((str[1] & 0x3f) << 6)
                        | (str[2] & 0x3f);
          str++;
