        {
          /* ignore invalid utf8? */
          if (len >= 2
              && ((unsigned char)str[1] < 0x80 || (unsigned char)str[1] > 0xBF
                  || (unsigned char)str[2] < 0x80
                  || (unsigned char)str[2] > 0xBF))
            {
              LOG_WARN ("utf-8: BAD_CONTINUATION_BYTE %s", str);
            }
          else if (len >= 1 && c == 0xe0 && (unsigned char)str[1] < 0xa0)
            {
              LOG_WARN ("utf-8: NON_SHORTEST %s", str);
            }
          else if (len >= 2)
            {
              wstr[i++] = ((c & 0x0f) << 12) | ((str[1] & 0x3f) << 6)
                | (str[2] & 0x3f);
              str++;
              str++;
              len--;
              len--;
            }
        }
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch changes the logic for handling 3-byte UTF-8 sequences by replacing standalone `if` statements with an `if-else if` chain. This ensures that if a warning condition for a bad sequence is met, the subsequent code block that performs the out-of-bounds write is not executed.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit c1ed1d91e28a6ddc7a9b5479d4795d58fb6be0ca
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType: Logic refactor (if-else chain)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review the 2-byte UTF-8 handling block (`(c & 0xe0) == 0xc0`) for similar fall-through logic issues.
- Fuzz other string conversion functions in the library for similar validation flaws.
- Audit the codebase for other instances where warning conditions do not prevent subsequent unsafe operations.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar logic errors in other UTF-8 parsing sections could lead to comparable out-of-bounds write vulnerabilities.

