[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2021-46896 / CWE-120:
Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: Not available in input — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
PX4/PX4-Autopilot (pre-commit ab300418b3bb8cc18fee1a9497d413b88c915e0a)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the MavlinkReceiver::handle_message_trajectory_representation_waypoints function, where the code copies trajectory data from a received MAVLink message into a local waypoint array. The loop iterates up to a fixed maximum number of points (vehicle_trajectory_waypoint_s::NUMBER_POINTS) without sufficiently constraining the number of points reported by the message (trajectory.valid_points). This allows an attacker to send a message with a valid_points value exceeding the destination array's capacity, leading to an out-of-bounds write during the copy operations.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The loop condition uses the destination array's maximum size instead of the minimum of that size and the source's valid_points field, allowing an unchecked buffer copy.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network MAVLink message handler
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service (DoS)
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A maliciously crafted MAVLink TRAJECTORY_REPRESENTATION_WAYPOINTS message.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c++
const int number_valid_points = trajectory.valid_points;

for (int i = 0; i < vehicle_trajectory_waypoint_s::NUMBER_POINTS; ++i) {
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].position[0] = trajectory.pos_x[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].position[1] = trajectory.pos_y[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].position[2] = trajectory.pos_z[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].velocity[0] = trajectory.vel_x[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].velocity[1] = trajectory.vel_y[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].velocity[2] = trajectory.vel_z[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].acceleration[0] = trajectory.acc_x[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].acceleration[1] = trajectory.acc_y[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].acceleration[2] = trajectory.acc_z[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].yaw = trajectory.pos_yaw[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].yaw_speed = trajectory.vel_yaw[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].type = UINT8_MAX;
}
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network MAVLink message handler

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can remotely trigger the vulnerability by sending a single MAVLink message with a large valid_points field, leading to a crash and a denial of service.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a MAVLink TRAJECTORY_REPRESENTATION_WAYPOINTS message.
- Set the valid_points field to a value greater than vehicle_trajectory_waypoint_s::NUMBER_POINTS.
- Populate the trajectory data arrays (e.g., pos_x, vel_y) with arbitrary data.
- Transmit the crafted message to the target system.
- The system processes the message, triggering the out-of-bounds write during the loop.
- The PX4 autopilot firmware crashes, resulting in a denial of service.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c++
const int number_valid_points = math::min(trajectory.valid_points, vehicle_trajectory_waypoint_s::NUMBER_POINTS);

for (int i = 0; i < number_valid_points; ++i) {
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].position[0] = trajectory.pos_x[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].position[1] = trajectory.pos_y[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].position[2] = trajectory.pos_z[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].velocity[0] = trajectory.vel_x[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].velocity[1] = trajectory.vel_y[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].velocity[2] = trajectory.vel_z[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].acceleration[0] = trajectory.acc_x[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].acceleration[1] = trajectory.acc_y[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].acceleration[2] = trajectory.acc_z[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].yaw = trajectory.pos_yaw[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].yaw_speed = trajectory.vel_yaw[i];
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].point_valid = true;
	trajectory_waypoint.waypoints[i].type = UINT8_MAX;
}
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch ensures the number of points to process is the lesser of the value reported in the message (trajectory.valid_points) and the static capacity of the destination array, preventing an out-of-bounds write.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/PX4/PX4-Autopilot/commit/ab300418b3bb8cc18fee1a9497d413b88c915e0a
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other MAVLink message handlers in the codebase that similarly copy array data based on a user-controlled length field.
- Fuzz the MAVLink parser with messages containing unexpectedly large array sizes or counts.
- Diff forks and related projects for similar patterns of copying data without proper bounds validation.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar buffer overflow vulnerabilities may exist in other MAVLink message parsers.

