files.wiretap/iseries.c:
static gboolean
iseries_check_file_type (wtap * wth, int *err, gchar **err_info, int format)
{
  gboolean   is_iseries = FALSE;
  guint      line;
  int        num_items_scanned;
  char       buf[ISERIES_LINE_LENGTH], protocol[9];
  iseries_t *iseries;

  /* Save trace format for passing between packets */
  iseries                = (iseries_t *) g_malloc (sizeof (iseries_t));
  iseries->have_date     = FALSE;
  iseries->format        = format;

  for (line = 0; line < ISERIES_HDR_LINES_TO_CHECK; line++)
    {
      memset(buf, 0x0, sizeof(buf));
      if (file_gets (buf, ISERIES_LINE_LENGTH, wth->fh) == NULL)
        {
          /* EOF or error. */
          *err = file_error (wth->fh, err_info);
          if (*err == WTAP_ERR_SHORT_READ)
            *err = 0;
          break;
        }

        /*
         * Check that we are dealing with an ETHERNET trace
         */
        if (iseries->format == ISERIES_FORMAT_UNICODE)
          {
            iseries_UNICODE_to_ASCII ((guint8 *)buf, ISERIES_LINE_LENGTH);
          }
        ascii_strup_inplace (buf);
        num_items_scanned = sscanf (buf,
                                   "%*[ \n\t]OBJECT PROTOCOL%*[ .:\n\t]%8s",
                                   protocol);
        if (num_items_scanned == 1)
          {
            if (memcmp (protocol, "ETHERNET", 8) == 0)
              {
                *err = 0;
                is_iseries = TRUE;
              }
          }

        /*
         * The header is the only place where the date part of the timestamp is held, so
         * extract it here and store for all packets to access
         */
        num_items_scanned = sscanf (buf,
                                    "%*[ \n\t]START DATE/TIME%*[ .:\n\t]%2d/%2d/%2d",
                                    &iseries->month, &iseries->day,
                                    &iseries->year);
        if (num_items_scanned == 3)
          {
            iseries->have_date = TRUE;
          }
    }

  if (is_iseries)
    wth->priv = (void *) iseries;
  else
    g_free(iseries);

  return is_iseries;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2016-2529

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2016-2529 / CWE-119:
Memory Corruption in iseries parser

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2016-02-28 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
 The vulnerability exists in the `iseries_check_file_type` function in `wiretap/iseries.c`. The function unconditionally assigns the allocated `iseries` structure to `wth->priv` before fully validating the file type, and fails to properly initialize the `buf` stack buffer. This can lead to memory corruption if the file is not an iSeries file, as the `wth->priv` pointer may point to freed or uninitialized memory, and parsing may use uninitialized data from `buf`.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 Unconditional assignment of a newly allocated structure to a context pointer and failure to initialize a stack buffer before use.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network capture file parser (Wireshark)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malformed iSeries trace file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

```c
  /* Save trace format for passing between packets */
  iseries                = (iseries_t *) g_malloc (sizeof (iseries_t));
  wth->priv              = (void *) iseries;
  iseries->have_date     = FALSE;
  iseries->format        = format;

  for (line = 0; line < ISERIES_HDR_LINES_TO_CHECK; line++)
    {
      if (file_gets (buf, ISERIES_LINE_LENGTH, wth->fh) == NULL)
        {
          /* EOF or error. */
          *err = file_error (wth->fh, err_info);
          if (*err == WTAP_ERR_SHORT_READ)
            *err = 0;
          return FALSE;
        }
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File parser

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Denial of Service via memory corruption

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can cause a denial of service by crafting a file that triggers the use of an invalid context pointer or uninitialized memory.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft a file that appears to be an iSeries trace initially but fails validation.
- Ensure the file contains a line that causes an early exit from the parsing loop.
- The program will exit with `wth->priv` pointing to a structure that will be freed, leading to a use-after-free or access to uninitialized memory.
- This results in a crash, causing a denial of service.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
  gboolean   is_iseries = FALSE;
  guint      line;
  int        num_items_scanned;
  char       buf[ISERIES_LINE_LENGTH], protocol[9];
  iseries_t *iseries;

  /* Save trace format for passing between packets */
  iseries                = (iseries_t *) g_malloc (sizeof (iseries_t));
  iseries->have_date     = FALSE;
  iseries->format        = format;

  for (line = 0; line < ISERIES_HDR_LINES_TO_CHECK; line++)
    {
      memset(buf, 0x0, sizeof(buf));
      if (file_gets (buf, ISERIES_LINE_LENGTH, wth->fh) == NULL)
        {
          /* EOF or error. */
          *err = file_error (wth->fh, err_info);
          if (*err == WTAP_ERR_SHORT_READ)
            *err = 0;
          break;
        }
      /* ... parsing logic ... */
      if (memcmp (protocol, "ETHERNET", 8) == 0)
        {
          *err = 0;
          is_iseries = TRUE;
        }
    }

  if (is_iseries)
    wth->priv = (void *) iseries;
  else
    g_free(iseries);

  return is_iseries;
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
 The patch adds a flag `is_iseries` to track successful validation, initializes the `buf` buffer to prevent reading uninitialized memory, and only assigns `wth->priv` after the file is confirmed to be a valid iSeries file. If validation fails, the allocated structure is freed.

[Optional] Patch reference:
 https://github.com/wireshark/wireshark/commit/96d585a5e9baef21e1eea8505d78305b034dc80e
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other file type parsers in Wireshark for similar patterns of early assignment of context pointers.
- Fuzz the iSeries parser and other similar parsers with files designed to hit early exit conditions.
- Diff the `wireshark` repository against forks to check if this pattern exists in unmaintained versions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar logic errors in other parsers could lead to denial of service or potential code execution.

