} else {
    PHAR_SET_16(eocd.counthere, zend_hash_num_elements(&phar->manifest));
    PHAR_SET_16(eocd.count, zend_hash_num_elements(&phar->manifest));
}
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File format (PHAR archive)

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Arbitrary read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger the vulnerability by inducing a victim to process a malicious PHAR archive, potentially leading to the disclosure of sensitive memory contents.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a malicious PHAR archive with manipulated metadata or stub lengths.
- Ensure the crafted archive causes the phar_zip_flush function to read past the intended buffer.
- Deliver the archive to the target application.
- The application processes the archive, triggering the out-of-bounds read.
- Information from adjacent memory may be leaked through application behavior or error messages.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
strncpy(eocd.signature, "PK\5\6", 4);
if (!phar->is_data && !phar->sig_flags) {
    phar->sig_flags = PHAR_SIG_SHA1;
}
if (phar->sig_flags) {
    PHAR_SET_16(eocd.counthere, zend_hash_num_elements(&phar->manifest) + 1);
    PHAR_SET_16(eocd.count, zend_hash_num_elements(&phar->manifest) + 1);
} else {
    PHAR_SET_16(eocd.counthere, zend_hash_num_elements(&phar->manifest));
    PHAR_SET_16(eocd.count, zend_hash_num_elements(&phar->manifest));
}
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix adds proper bounds checking to prevent out-of-bounds read operations when processing PHAR archive metadata, ensuring that data access is confined within valid buffer limits.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Not available in input

