[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2016-0838

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2016-0838 / CWE-119:
Memory corruption in WT_InterpolateNoLoop

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2016-04-18 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `WT_InterpolateNoLoop` function within the Sonivox audio engine. The function processes audio samples based on a `numSamples` count from a controlled input structure. While there is a check for `numSamples <= 0`, there is no upper bound check, allowing an attacker to specify a large value. This causes a loop to write past the end of the output buffer (`pOutputBuffer`), leading to heap memory corruption.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The loop that writes to the output buffer is controlled by an attacker-supplied `numSamples` value without an upper bounds check.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Local audio processing / media framework
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Memory corruption → Potential remote code execution
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious audio file or crafted data processed by the Sonivox engine.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
    /* initialize some local variables */
    numSamples = pWTIntFrame->numSamples;
    if (numSamples <= 0) {
        ALOGE("b/26366256");
        android_errorWriteLog(0x534e4554, "26366256");
        return;
    }
    pOutputBuffer = pWTIntFrame->pAudioBuffer;
    /* ... */
    while (numSamples--) {
        /* save new output sample in buffer */
        *pOutputBuffer++ = (EAS_I16)(acc0 >> 2);
        /* ... */
    }
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Malicious media file processed by the Sonivox library.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write on the heap.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is highly exploitable as an attacker can control the size and data of an out-of-bounds write on the heap by providing a crafted audio file.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker creates a specially crafted audio file.
- The file is processed by an application using the vulnerable Sonivox library.
- The parser sets `pWTIntFrame->numSamples` to a large value.
- The `while` loop in `WT_InterpolateNoLoop` writes past the allocated `pAudioBuffer`.
- This corrupts adjacent heap structures, potentially leading to code execution.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
    /* initialize some local variables */
    numSamples = pWTIntFrame->numSamples;
    if (numSamples <= 0) {
        ALOGE("b/26366256");
        android_errorWriteLog(0x534e4554, "26366256");
        return;
    }
    if (numSamples > MAX_EXPECTED_SAMPLES) {
        ALOGE("b/26366256");
        android_errorWriteLog(0x534e4554, "26366256");
        return;
    }
    pOutputBuffer = pWTIntFrame->pAudioBuffer;
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix adds an upper bounds check on the `numSamples` variable to prevent the loop from writing past the end of the output buffer, mitigating the heap corruption vulnerability.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit 24d7c408c52143bce7b49de82f3913fd8d1219cf
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the Sonivox engine that use `numSamples` or similar loop counters from external input.
- Fuzz the audio file parsers to identify any other missing bounds checks.
- Audit other media processing components for similar patterns of using untrusted loop counters.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar buffer overflow vulnerabilities may exist in other audio processing functions.

END INSTRUCTION.

