[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2016-1653

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2016-1653 / CWE-119:
LoadBuffer typing assumption

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2016-04-18 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Chromium — pre-2016-04-18 patch

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Mandatory] Summary:
A flaw exists in the `LoadBuffer` operator within the V8 JavaScript engine's simplified lowering phase. The code assumes that `LoadBuffer` will only return a value of the specified typed array type, but out-of-bounds access can cause it to return `undefined` or `NaN`. This incorrect type assumption is propagated through the compiler, leading to the generation of incorrect machine code, such as feeding `NaN` into `ChangeFloat64ToUint32` and producing an unexpected integer result.

[Mandatory] Root cause:
The compiler's type system incorrectly assumes the output of `LoadBuffer` is strictly the element type of the buffer, failing to account for `undefined` from out-of-bounds loads.

[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
JavaScript engine (V8), specifically processing asm.js-style heap access.

[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Incorrect code generation → potential information leak or logic bypass

[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious JavaScript code that performs an out-of-bounds access on an asm.js heap buffer.

[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```cpp
      case IrOpcode::kLoadBuffer: {
        BufferAccess access = BufferAccessOf(node->op());
        ProcessInput(node, 0, UseInfo::PointerInt());        // buffer
        ProcessInput(node, 1, UseInfo::TruncatingWord32());  // offset
        ProcessInput(node, 2, UseInfo::TruncatingWord32());  // length
        ProcessRemainingInputs(node, 3);

        NodeOutputInfo output_info;
        if (truncation.TruncatesUndefinedToZeroOrNaN()) {
          if (truncation.TruncatesNaNToZero()) {
            output_info = NodeOutputInfo(access.machine_type().representation(),
                                         NodeProperties::GetType(node));
          } else {
            if (access.machine_type().representation() !=
                MachineRepresentation::kFloat64) {
              output_info = NodeOutputInfo::Float64();
            }
          }
        } else {
          output_info = NodeOutputInfo::AnyTagged();
        }
        SetOutput(node, output_info);
        if (lower())
          lowering->DoLoadBuffer(node, output_info.representation(), changer_);
        break;
      }
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
JavaScript execution in the V8 engine.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Type confusion, incorrect value truncation.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can craft JavaScript to trigger an out-of-bounds `LoadBuffer`, causing the compiler to produce incorrect code that mishandles `NaN` values, potentially leading to further exploitation.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft JavaScript that creates an array buffer and a typed array view.
- Perform an out-of-bounds read operation on the typed array.
- The read operation returns `undefined` or `NaN`.
- The V8 compiler's `LoadBuffer` node incorrectly assumes a valid typed value.
- This incorrect assumption propagates, leading to a bug in later operations like `ChangeFloat64ToUint32`.
- The resulting incorrect integer value can be used to subvert program logic.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```cpp
      case IrOpcode::kLoadBuffer: {
        BufferAccess access = BufferAccessOf(node->op());
        ProcessInput(node, 0, UseInfo::PointerInt());        // buffer
        ProcessInput(node, 1, UseInfo::TruncatingWord32());  // offset
        ProcessInput(node, 2, UseInfo::TruncatingWord32());  // length
        ProcessRemainingInputs(node, 3);

        NodeOutputInfo output_info;
        if (truncation.TruncatesUndefinedToZeroOrNaN()) {
          if (truncation.TruncatesNaNToZero()) {
            output_info = NodeOutputInfo(access.machine_type().representation(),
                                         NodeProperties::GetType(node));
          } else {
            if (access.machine_type().representation() !=
                MachineRepresentation::kFloat64) {
              // TODO(bmeurer): See comment on abort_compilation_.
              if (lower()) lowering->abort_compilation_ = true;
            }
            output_info = NodeOutputInfo::Float64();
          }
        } else {
          // TODO(bmeurer): See comment on abort_compilation_.
          if (lower()) lowering->abort_compilation_ = true;

          // If undefined is not truncated away, we need to have the tagged
          // representation.
          output_info = NodeOutputInfo::AnyTagged();
        }
        SetOutput(node, output_info);
        if (lower())
          lowering->DoLoadBuffer(node, output_info.representation(), changer_);
        break;
      }
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds checks to abort the compilation pipeline if the typing assumptions for `LoadBuffer` do not hold, preventing incorrect code generation when out-of-bounds access could result in `undefined` or `NaN`.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit 58ab990aa8c3aeee38e888c1c33404f4b5a14759

[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
The fix introduces bailout logic (`lowering->abort_compilation_ = true;`) when the type system's assumptions about `LoadBuffer`'s output are violated. This is a logic change to handle edge cases rather than a simple bounds check.

[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other operators that make similar assumptions about input or output types, especially those interacting with memory buffers.
- Fuzz the JavaScript engine with code that intentionally causes type mismatches, such as mixing integer and floating-point operations on buffer data.
- Diff forks or older versions of the codebase for similar `LoadBuffer` or `StoreBuffer` patterns that may lack this fix.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other memory access operators with flawed type assumptions could lead to similar incorrect code generation vulnerabilities.

