[Mandatory] ID: CVE-2016-3134

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2016-3134 / CWE-119:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2016-04-27 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Linux kernel (versions prior to commit 54d83fc74aa9ec72794373cb47432c5f7fb1a309)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
A bug exists in the `check_underflow` function in net/ipv4/netfilter/arp_tables.c. The function incorrectly determines if a rule is unconditional by passing `&e->arp` to the `unconditional` helper, instead of the full entry `e`. This allows a rule with matches to be incorrectly classified as unconditional, potentially leading to out-of-bounds memory writes in `mark_source_chains` when processing a crafted netfilter rule.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Incorrect argument (`&e->arp`) passed to the `unconditional()` helper function, causing a misclassification of rule conditions.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel netfilter subsystem.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local Privilege Escalation
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Crafted netfilter rule via a system call with elevated privileges.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static bool check_underflow(const struct arpt_entry *e)
{
	const struct xt_entry_target *t;
	unsigned int verdict;

	if (!unconditional(&e->arp))
		return false;
	t = arpt_get_target_c(e);
	if (strcmp(t->u.user.name, XT_STANDARD_TARGET) != 0)
		return false;
	verdict = ((struct xt_standard_target *)t)->verdict;
	verdict = -verdict - 1;
	return verdict == NF_DROP || verdict == NF_ACCEPT;
}

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Netfilter rule addition system call.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is exploitable by a local user with the CAP_NET_ADMIN capability who can create a specially crafted iptables rule, leading to a kernel memory corruption and potential privilege escalation.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker requires CAP_NET_ADMIN capability.
- An iptables rule is crafted that appears unconditional to `check_underflow` but contains matches.
- This rule is added to the `arp_tables`.
- The flawed logic in `check_underflow` allows the rule to pass validation.
- This leads to an out-of-bounds write in `mark_source_chains` when processing the rule's `next_offset`.
- The memory corruption can be leveraged to gain root privileges.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static bool check_underflow(const struct arpt_entry *e)
{
	const struct xt_entry_target *t;
	unsigned int verdict;

	if (!unconditional(e))
		return false;
	t = arpt_get_target_c(e);
	if (strcmp(t->u.user.name, XT_STANDARD_TARGET) != 0)
		return false;
	verdict = ((struct xt_standard_target *)t)->verdict;
	verdict = -verdict - 1;
	return verdict == NF_DROP || verdict == NF_ACCEPT;
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch corrects the argument passed to the `unconditional()` function from `&e->arp` to `e`, ensuring the helper correctly evaluates the entire rule entry for conditions.
