annotation:
[Mandatory] ID: CVE-2016-3134

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2016-3134 / CWE-119:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2016-04-27 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `check_underflow` function within `net/ipv4/netfilter/ip_tables.c`. The function incorrectly determines if a netfilter rule is unconditional by passing the entire `ipt_entry` structure `e` to the `unconditional()` function. The original code passed `&e->ip`, which led to a mismatch in how unconditional rules were validated compared to other parts of the code, like `mark_source_chains`. This inconsistency could allow a user-supplied `ipt_entry` with a large `next_offset` field to pass the underflow check, leading to an out-of-bounds write when `mark_source_chains` attempts to write a counter value at the malicious offset.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Incorrect argument passed to the `unconditional()` helper function, causing inconsistent validation of unconditional rules.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel netfilter subsystem.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Maliciously crafted iptables rule.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static bool check_underflow(const struct ipt_entry *e)
{
	const struct xt_entry_target *t;
	unsigned int verdict;

	if (!unconditional(e))
		return false;
	t = ipt_get_target_c(e);
	if (strcmp(t->u.user.name, XT_STANDARD_TARGET) != 0)
		return false;
	verdict = ((struct xt_standard_target *)t)->verdict;
	verdict = -verdict - 1;
	return verdict == NF_DROP || verdict == NF_ACCEPT;
}

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
iptables rule configuration.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is triggered by a local user with sufficient privileges to modify netfilter rules, leading to a kernel out-of-bounds write and potential privilege escalation.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker requires privileges to add iptables rules.
- An iptables rule is crafted that appears unconditional to `check_underflow` but not to `mark_source_chains`.
- The rule's `next_offset` field is set to a large, malicious value.
- When the rule is processed, `mark_source_chains` writes beyond allocated memory.
- This results in kernel memory corruption, potentially leading to privilege escalation.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static bool check_underflow(const struct ipt_entry *e)
{
	const struct xt_entry_target *t;
	unsigned int verdict;

	if (!unconditional(e))
		return false;
	t = ipt_get_target_c(e);
	if (strcmp(t->u.user.name, XT_STANDARD_TARGET) != 0)
		return false;
	verdict = ((struct xt_standard_target *)t)->verdict;
	verdict = -verdict - 1;
	return verdict == NF_DROP || verdict == NF_ACCEPT;
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix changes the argument passed to the `unconditional()` function from `&e->ip` to `e` to ensure consistent validation of unconditional rules across the codebase.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/54d83fc74aa9ec72794373cb47432c5f7fb1a309
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- Note: Logic refactor to unify the definition of an 'unconditional rule'.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other callers of the `unconditional()` helper for similar inconsistencies.
- Fuzz the iptables rule parsing logic with malformed `ipt_entry` structures.
- Diff against older kernel versions and forks to check for the presence of the incorrect call pattern.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Other netfilter subsystems (ip6tables, arptables) that use a similar helper may be affected.

