[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2015-8868

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2015-8868 / CWE-119:
Buffer overflow in ExponentialFunction constructor

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2016-05-06 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
poppler (pre-2015-12-22 patch)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
The ExponentialFunction constructor in poppler/Function.cc can be triggered to write past the end of the `c0` array. This occurs when processing a crafted PDF where the "C0" array length is larger than the `funcMaxOutputs` limit. The code sets `n` to the attacker-controlled array length without sufficient bounds checking before using `n` as a loop counter to write into the fixed-size `c0` buffer.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The loop that writes into the `c0` array uses an unchecked, attacker-controlled value `n` as its upper bound.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
PDF parser (poppler library)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Arbitrary code execution via heap-based buffer overflow.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Crafted PDF file containing a malicious ExponentialFunction dictionary.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
    n = obj1.arrayGetLength();
    for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
      obj1.arrayGet(i, &obj2);
      if (!obj2.isNum()) {
	error(errSyntaxError, -1, "Illegal value in function C0 array");
	goto err3;
      }
      c0[i] = obj2.getNum();
      obj2.free();
    }

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
PDF file parsing via ExponentialFunction constructor.
[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write on the heap.
[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is highly exploitable as an attacker can control the size and data written past the buffer, potentially corrupting heap metadata to achieve code execution.
[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a PDF with an ExponentialFunction dictionary.
- Define a "C0" array with a length greater than `funcMaxOutputs`.
- Populate the "C0" array with numeric values.
- When poppler processes the file, it will write these values past the `c0` buffer.
- This results in a heap-based buffer overflow.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
    n = obj1.arrayGetLength();
    if (unlikely(n > funcMaxOutputs)) {
      error(errSyntaxError, -1, "Function's C0 array is wrong length");
      n = funcMaxOutputs;
    }
    for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
      obj1.arrayGet(i, &obj2);
      if (!obj2.isNum()) {
	error(errSyntaxError, -1, "Illegal value in function C0 array");
	goto err3;
      }
      c0[i] = obj2.getNum();
      obj2.free();
    }

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a bounds check to ensure that `n`, the length of the "C0" array, does not exceed the maximum allowed outputs (`funcMaxOutputs`). If it does, `n` is capped to `funcMaxOutputs` to prevent a buffer overflow.
[Optional] Patch reference:
https://cgit.freedesktop.org/poppler/poppler/commit/?id=b3425dd3261679958cd56c0f71995c15d2124433
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other function constructors (e.g., StitchingFunction, SampledFunction) for similar missing bounds checks on array lengths.
- Fuzz PDF files with large array sizes in other function-type dictionaries to identify latent overflows.
- Audit all uses of `arrayGetLength()` that directly influence loop bounds or memory allocation sizes.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar buffer overflows may exist in other PDF function parsers.

