MG_INTERNAL int parse_mqtt(struct mbuf *io, struct mg_mqtt_message *mm) {
  uint8_t header;
  size_t len = 0, len_len = 0;
  const char *p, *end, *eop = &io->buf[io->len];
  unsigned char lc = 0;
  int cmd;

  if (io->len < 2) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_INCOMPLETE_MSG;
  header = io->buf[0];
  cmd = header >> 4;

  /* decode mqtt variable length */
  len = len_len = 0;
  p = io->buf + 1;
  while (p < eop) {
    lc = *((const unsigned char *) p++);
    len += (lc & 0x7f) << 7 * len_len;
    len_len++;
    if (!(lc & 0x80)) break;
    if (len_len > 4) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
  }

  end = p + len;
  if (lc & 0x80 || end > eop) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_INCOMPLETE_MSG;

  mm->cmd = cmd;
  mm->qos = MG_MQTT_GET_QOS(header);

  switch (cmd) {
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_CONNECT: {
      p = scanto(p, &mm->protocol_name);
      if (p > end - 4) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      mm->protocol_version = *(uint8_t *) p++;
      mm->connect_flags = *(uint8_t *) p++;
      mm->keep_alive_timer = getu16(p);
      p += 2;
      if (p >= end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      p = scanto(p, &mm->client_id);
      if (p > end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      if (mm->connect_flags & MG_MQTT_HAS_WILL) {
        if (p >= end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
        p = scanto(p, &mm->will_topic);
      }
      if (mm->connect_flags & MG_MQTT_HAS_WILL) {
        if (p >= end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
        p = scanto(p, &mm->will_message);
      }
      if (mm->connect_flags & MG_MQTT_HAS_USER_NAME) {
        if (p >= end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
        p = scanto(p, &mm->user_name);
      }
      if (mm->connect_flags & MG_MQTT_HAS_PASSWORD) {
        if (p >= end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
        p = scanto(p, &mm->password);
      }
      if (p != end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;

      LOG(LL_DEBUG,
          ("%d %2x %d proto [%.*s] client_id [%.*s] will_topic [%.*s] "
           "will_msg [%.*s] user_name [%.*s] password [%.*s]",
           (int) len, (int) mm->connect_flags, (int) mm->keep_alive_timer,
           (int) mm->protocol_name.len, mm->protocol_name.p,
           (int) mm->client_id.len, mm->client_id.p, (int) mm->will_topic.len,
           mm->will_topic.p, (int) mm->will_message.len, mm->will_message.p,
           (int) mm->user_name.len, mm->user_name.p, (int) mm->password.len,
           mm->password.p));
      break;
    }
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_CONNACK:
      if (end - p < 2) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      mm->connack_ret_code = p[1];
      break;
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_PUBACK:
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_PUBREC:
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_PUBREL:
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_PUBCOMP:
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_SUBACK:
      if (end - p < 2) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      mm->message_id = getu16(p);
      p += 2;
      break;
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_PUBLISH: {
      p = scanto(p, &mm->topic);
      if (p > end) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      if (mm->qos > 0) {
        if (end - p < 2) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
        mm->message_id = getu16(p);
        p += 2;
      }
      mm->payload.p = p;
      mm->payload.len = end - p;
      break;
    }
    case MG_MQTT_CMD_SUBSCRIBE:
      if (end - p < 2) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
      mm->message_id = getu16(p);
      p += 2;
      /*
       * topic expressions are left in the payload and can be parsed with
       * `mg_mqtt_next_subscribe_topic`
       */
      mm->payload.p = p;
      mm->payload.len = end - p;
      break;
    default:
      /* Unhandled command */
      break;
  }

  mm->len = end - io->buf;
  return mm->len;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2019-12951

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2019-12951 / CWE-787:
Out-of-bounds write in MQTT parser

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: Not available in input — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-787

[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `parse_mqtt` function in `src/mg_mqtt.c`. When parsing an MQTT message, the code calculates the message length and determines the end pointer. However, the original bounds check `len > (io->len - (p - io->buf))` is insufficient and can be bypassed, allowing `end = p + len` to point beyond the actual buffer. Subsequent operations, such as reading a 16-bit integer in the `MG_MQTT_CMD_SUBACK` case, can then write data out of bounds, leading to a heap-based buffer overflow.

[Mandatory] Root cause:
An insufficient bounds check on the decoded MQTT message length allows the calculated end pointer to exceed the buffer's limits.

[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network service (MQTT parser)

[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Remote Code Execution / Denial of Service

[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Specially crafted MQTT message sent over the network.

[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
  /* decode mqtt variable length */
  len = len_len = 0;
  p = io->buf + 1;
  while ((size_t)(p - io->buf) < io->len) {
    lc = *((const unsigned char *) p++);
    len += (lc & 0x7f) << 7 * len_len;
    len_len++;
    if (!(lc & 0x80)) break;
    if (len_len > 4) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
  }

  end = p + len;
  if (lc & 0x80 || len > (io->len - (p - io->buf))) {
    return MG_MQTT_ERROR_INCOMPLETE_MSG;
  }
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network message containing a maliciously encoded MQTT length field.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write (heap corruption)

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is highly exploitable as it can be triggered remotely by sending a single malformed MQTT packet, leading to heap corruption that can potentially be leveraged for remote code execution.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker connects to the MQTT service.
- Attacker sends an MQTT packet where the variable length integer is crafted to be large.
- The parser's length check is bypassed, causing an `end` pointer to be calculated past the buffer's end.
- A subsequent operation, like processing a `SUBACK` command, reads from this out-of-bounds location.
- This results in a write operation outside the allocated buffer, corrupting the heap.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
  const char *p, *end, *eop = &io->buf[io->len];
  ...
  /* decode mqtt variable length */
  len = len_len = 0;
  p = io->buf + 1;
  while (p < eop) {
    lc = *((const unsigned char *) p++);
    len += (lc & 0x7f) << 7 * len_len;
    len_len++;
    if (!(lc & 0x80)) break;
    if (len_len > 4) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_MALFORMED_MSG;
  }

  end = p + len;
  if (lc & 0x80 || end > eop) return MG_MQTT_ERROR_INCOMPLETE_MSG;
```

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch introduces a pointer `eop` that marks the end of the input buffer and replaces the complex arithmetic bounds check with a simple pointer comparison (`end > eop`), ensuring the calculated `end` never exceeds the buffer.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit b3e0f780c34cea88f057a62213c012aa88fe2deb

[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- Field: PatchType (bounds check added, logic refactor)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other parts of the MQTT parser for similar arithmetic-based bounds checks that could be replaced with safer pointer comparisons.
- Fuzz the MQTT message parser with a focus on malformed variable-length integers to ensure no other overflow paths exist.
- Audit other protocol parsers within the codebase for analogous length validation weaknesses.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds write vulnerabilities could exist in other message parsers that rely on length fields without strict pointer-based validation.

