[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2019-13067

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2019-13067 / CWE-125:
Out-of-bounds read in njs_string_replace_regexp

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2019-06-30 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
nginx/njs (pre-f15fb784bbc537917cf59a5de922131fc5c18431)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-125: Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
A bug exists in the `njs_string_replace_regexp` function in `njs/njs_string.c`. When handling an empty match during a global regular expression replacement with a function, the code incorrectly calculates the size of subsequent string parts. This can lead to an out-of-bounds read when `njs_string_replace_regexp_function` is called after the `r->part[0]` assignment, because the state of `r->part` and its sizes are inconsistent.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
Missing bounds check and incorrect state management after an empty match.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
JavaScript engine (njs) processing regular expression replacements.
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Information leak or potential denial of service due to out-of-bounds read.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
A JavaScript string provided to njs that triggers a global regular expression replacement with an empty match and a replacer function.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
                if (captures[1] == 0) {

                    /* Empty match. */

                    start = r->part[0].start;

                    if (start < end) {
                        p = (u_char *) nxt_utf8_next(start, end);

                        r->part[1].start = start;
                        r->part[1].size = p - start;

                        r->part[2].start = p;
                        r->part[2].size = end - p;

                    } else {
                        r->part[1].size = 0;
                        r->part[2].size = 0;

                        /* To exit the loop. */
                        r->part[2].start = start + 1;
                    }

                    r->part[0] = replace;
                }
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
njs JavaScript engine API for string replacement.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read.

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
Exploitable by providing a crafted input string to the njs engine that forces an out-of-bounds memory read during a specific regex replacement operation.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a JavaScript string that will result in an empty global regex match.
- Provide a replacer function as the second argument to the `replace` method.
- Invoke the njs engine to execute the replacement.
- The incorrect handling of the empty match will trigger an out-of-bounds read.
- This can lead to an information leak or crash.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
                if (captures[1] == 0) {

                    /* Empty match. */

                    start = r->part[0].start;

                    if (start < end) {
                        p = (u_char *) nxt_utf8_next(start, end);

                        r->part[1].start = start;
                        r->part[1].size = p - start;

                        r->part[2].start = p;
                        r->part[2].size = end - p;

                    } else {
                        r->part[1].size = 0;
                        r->part[2].size = 0;

                        /* To exit the loop. */
                        r->part[2].start = start + 1;
                    }

                    if (r->function != NULL) {
                        return njs_string_replace_regexp_function(vm, args, r,
                                                                 captures, ret);
                    }

                    r->part[0] = replace;
                }
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch moves the call to `njs_string_replace_regexp_function` to occur before `r->part[0]` is overwritten with the replacement value. This ensures the function receives the correct, unmodified match context, preventing the out-of-bounds read.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit f15fb784bbc537917cf59a5de922131fc5c18431
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (logic refactor)
The fix reordered operations within the `if (captures[1] == 0)` block to handle the replacer function call before modifying the `r->part` array, thus preserving the correct state for the function call.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other string manipulation functions in njs for similar logic errors where state is modified before a dependent function call.
- Fuzz the njs regex engine with a focus on global replacements involving empty matches and replacer functions.
- Diff forks or older versions of njs to check for the presence of this vulnerable pattern.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar logic errors in other complex string parsing or modification routines could lead to comparable out-of-bounds issues.

