[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2019-13290

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2019-13290 / CWE-787:
Out-of-bounds write in display list node allocation

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2019-07-04 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
ArtifexSoftware/mupdf (commit ed19bc806809ad10c4ddce515d375581b86ede85 and prior)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability is in the `fz_append_display_node` function in `source/fitz/list-device.c`. When adding a new node to a display list, the code checks if the list has enough capacity with an `if` statement. If the required size is larger than the remaining space, the list's buffer is reallocated and doubled only once. However, if the size of the node to be appended is larger than the newly allocated space, the subsequent write to the list buffer will exceed its bounds, leading to an out-of-bounds write. This is exploitable because an attacker can control the size of the appended node, for instance via a large layer name string, causing a heap overflow.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The allocation logic uses a single conditional check and one-time doubling, which fails to ensure the buffer is large enough to hold an arbitrarily large node.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Document parser (MuPDF library)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Heap corruption leading to potential code execution
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Malicious document (e.g., PDF) with a large display list node
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
	if (list->len + size > list->max)
	{
		int newsize = list->max * 2;
		fz_display_node *old = list->list;
		ptrdiff_t diff;
		int i, n;

		if (newsize < 256)
			newsize = 256;
		list->list = fz_realloc_array(ctx, list->list, newsize, fz_display_node);
		list->max = newsize;
		diff = (char *)(list->list) - (char *)old;
		n = (writer->top < STACK_SIZE ? writer->top : STACK_SIZE);
		for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
		{
			if (writer->stack[i].update != NULL)
				writer->stack[i].update = (fz_rect *)(((char *)writer->stack[i].update) + diff);
		}
		if (writer->path)
			writer->path = (fz_path *)(((char *)writer->path) + diff);
	}
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Malicious document file processed by the MuPDF library.

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Heap out-of-bounds write

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The vulnerability is reliably exploitable by providing a document that forces the allocation of a display list node larger than twice the current buffer size, leading to a predictable heap overflow.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft a document (e.g., PDF) that includes a display command with a large private data payload or path data.
- The payload size must be larger than the current display list buffer's remaining capacity.
- Trigger the parsing of the crafted document using MuPDF.
- The `fz_append_display_node` function will be called with a large `size`.
- The initial reallocation will double the buffer size, but this may still be insufficient.
- The subsequent write operation will corrupt heap memory beyond the allocated buffer.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
	while (list->len + size > list->max)
	{
		int newsize = list->max * 2;
		fz_display_node *old = list->list;
		ptrdiff_t diff;
		int i, n;

		if (newsize < 256)
			newsize = 256;
		list->list = fz_realloc_array(ctx, list->list, newsize, fz_display_node);
		list->max = newsize;
		diff = (char *)(list->list) - (char *)old;
		n = (writer->top < STACK_SIZE ? writer->top : STACK_SIZE);
		for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
		{
			if (writer->stack[i].update != NULL)
				writer->stack[i].update = (fz_rect *)(((char *)writer->stack[i].update) + diff);
		}
		if (writer->path)
			writer->path = (fz_path *)(((char *)writer->path) + diff);
	}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch changes the `if` condition to a `while` loop, ensuring the list buffer is repeatedly reallocated and doubled until it is large enough to hold the new node, regardless of its size.

[Optional] Patch reference:
Commit ed19bc806809ad10c4ddce515d375581b86ede85 on ArtifexSoftware/mupdf
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType: bounds check added (via looped reallocation)
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other parts of the codebase that allocate buffers based on externally-controlled sizes to ensure they use looped reallocation or pre-calculate the required size.
- Fuzz the document parser with inputs designed to trigger large allocations in various display list handling functions.
- Audit similar functions that might use a single reallocation step for growing buffers.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar single-step reallocation logic in other parts of the library could lead to comparable heap overflow vulnerabilities.

