annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2019-13307

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2019-13307 / CWE-787:
Out-of-bounds write

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2019-07-05 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
ImageMagick (pre-2019-07-05 patch)

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `AcquirePixelThreadSet` function in `MagickCore/statistic.c`. The function allocates memory for a set of pixel channels based on the number of threads or image list length, but it incorrectly calculates the `columns` variable to be used for the inner allocation. It initializes `columns` to `MaxPixelChannels` and then expands it based on `next->columns`, which can exceed the size of the `pixels[i][j].channel` array (`MaxPixelChannels`). This leads to an out-of-bounds write when `pixels[i][j].channel[k]=0.0;` is executed with an index `k` that is out of range.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The `columns` variable used for memory allocation and subsequent indexing can exceed the fixed size of the inner `channel` array (`MaxPixelChannels`), leading to an out-of-bounds write.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Image processing library (ImageMagick)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Out-of-bounds write → Potential Remote Code Execution or Denial of Service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Specially crafted image file processed by ImageMagick
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
  columns=MaxPixelChannels;
  for (next=images; next != (Image *) NULL; next=next->next)
    columns=MagickMax(next->columns,columns);
  for (i=0; i < (ssize_t) rows; i++)
  {
    register ssize_t
      j;

    pixels[i]=(PixelChannels *) AcquireQuantumMemory(columns,sizeof(**pixels));
    if (pixels[i] == (PixelChannels *) NULL)
      return(DestroyPixelThreadSet(pixels));
    for (j=0; j < (ssize_t) columns; j++)
    {
      register ssize_t
        k;

      for (k=0; k < MaxPixelChannels; k++)
        pixels[i][j].channel[k]=0.0;
    }
  }
```

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File parsing via ImageMagick library
[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write
[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger this vulnerability by providing a malicious image with a large number of columns, leading to a controlled out-of-bounds write.
[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Create a malicious image file.
- Ensure the image's `columns` value is greater than `MaxPixelChannels`.
- Process the image with a vulnerable version of ImageMagick.
- The `AcquirePixelThreadSet` function will be triggered.
- An out-of-bounds write occurs at `pixels[i][j].channel[k]=0.0;`.
- This can corrupt memory and potentially lead to code execution.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
  rows=MagickMax(GetImageListLength(images),
    (size_t) GetMagickResourceLimit(ThreadResource));
  pixels=(PixelChannels **) AcquireQuantumMemory(rows,sizeof(*pixels));
  if (pixels == (PixelChannels **) NULL)
    return((PixelChannels **) NULL);
  columns=MaxPixelChannels;
  for (next=images; next != (Image *) NULL; next=next->next)
    columns=MagickMax(next->columns,columns);
  for (i=0; i < (ssize_t) rows; i++)
  {
    register ssize_t
      j;

    pixels[i]=(PixelChannels *) AcquireQuantumMemory(columns,sizeof(**pixels));
    if (pixels[i] == (PixelChannels *) NULL)
      return(DestroyPixelThreadSet(pixels));
    for (j=0; j < (ssize_t) columns; j++)
    {
      register ssize_t
        k;

      for (k=0; k < MaxPixelChannels; k++)
        pixels[i][j].channel[k]=0.0;
    }
  }
  return(pixels);
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The fix ensures that the `columns` variable is initialized to `MaxPixelChannels` and then potentially increased based on image columns, but the critical out-of-bounds write loop remains. However, the provided `diff_func` indicates the original vulnerable code used `images->columns` without considering other images in the list. The fix changes the allocation size for `pixels` from `number_threads` to `rows` (which is the max of thread count and image list length) and initializes `columns` to `MaxPixelChannels` before iterating through images to find the maximum width. This change aligns the allocation logic more closely with the usage, but the core OOB write in the innermost loop persists if `columns` exceeds `MaxPixelChannels`. The provided fix code is the post-patch version, which still contains the logical flaw described in the root cause; a more complete fix would involve changing the inner loop condition from `k < MaxPixelChannels` to `k < columns` or ensuring `columns` never exceeds `MaxPixelChannels`. Given the input, the minimal fix derived is to use the corrected allocation logic for `rows` and `columns` as shown.
[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/ImageMagick/ImageMagick/commit/025e77fcb2f45b21689931ba3bf74eac153afa48
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
Logic refactor: The allocation for the top-level array was changed to use `rows` (calculated from image list length and thread limit) instead of just the thread limit, and the calculation of `columns` was modified to start from `MaxPixelChannels`.
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the ImageMagick codebase that perform similar multi-dimensional allocations based on image dimensions.
- Fuzz image parsers that handle multiple images in a list to ensure similar miscalculations of dimensions do not exist.
- Diff forks of ImageMagick to see if they have merged this specific commit or have introduced similar logic errors.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar allocation logic errors could lead to other out-of-bounds read/write vulnerabilities in image processing functions.

