The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
The vulnerability exists in the `chmd_read_headers` function within `libmspack/mspack/chmd.c`. When processing system file entries in a CHM file, the code performs a series of `memcmp` operations on substrings of the filename without first verifying that the filename is long enough. This can lead to out-of-bounds memory reads if a maliciously crafted CHM file contains a system file entry with a name shorter than the expected length, potentially causing a crash or information disclosure.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The code performs memory comparisons on filename substrings without validating the total length of the filename against the size of the compared literals.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
File parser (CHM format)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service or Potential Information Disclosure
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Maliciously crafted CHM file
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):

      if (name[0] == ':' && name[1] == ':') {
        /* system file */
        if (memcmp(&name[2], &content_name[2], 31L) == 0) {
          if (memcmp(&name[33], &content_name[33], 8L) == 0) {
            chm->sec1.content = fi;
          }
          else if (memcmp(&name[33], &control_name[33], 11L) == 0) {
            chm->sec1.control = fi;
          }
          else if (memcmp(&name[33], &spaninfo_name[33], 8L) == 0) {
            chm->sec1.spaninfo = fi;
          }
          else if (memcmp(&name[33], &rtable_name[33], 72L) == 0) {
            chm->sec1.rtable = fi;
          }
        }
        fi->next = chm->sysfiles;
        chm->sysfiles = fi;
      }

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
File parser (CHM format)

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
An attacker can trigger the vulnerability by providing a CHM file with a system file entry name shorter than the length required for the subsequent `memcmp` operations, leading to predictable out-of-bounds reads.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft a CHM file containing a directory entry.
- Set the entry's name to start with "::" to be processed as a system file.
- Ensure the name's length is less than 105 bytes but greater than 2 bytes.
- The `memcmp` operations will then read past the end of the allocated name buffer.
- This results in a crash or potential leak of memory contents.

