static bool
addPayloadToBuffer(CotpConnection* self, uint8_t* buffer,  int payloadLength)
{
    if (payloadLength < 1) {
        if (DEBUG_COTP)
            printf("COTP: missing payload\n");

        return false;
    }

    if (DEBUG_COTP)
        printf("COTP: add to payload buffer (cur size: %i, len: %i)\n", self->payload->size, payloadLength);

    if ((self->payload->size + payloadLength) > self->payload->maxSize)
        return false;

    memcpy(self->payload->buffer + self->payload->size, buffer, payloadLength);

    self->payload->size += payloadLength;

    return true;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2019-1010300

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2019-1010300 / CWE-119:
Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2019-07-15 — Patch release: Not available in input

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Not available in input

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
The bug is in the `addPayloadToBuffer` function in `src/mms/iso_cotp/cotp.c`. It can be triggered by providing a payload of negative length, which bypasses the subsequent size check and leads to an out-of-bounds write via `memcpy`. This is exploitable because the function does not validate that the `payloadLength` argument is non-negative before using it in memory operations.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
The function lacks a check to ensure `payloadLength` is non-negative before performing arithmetic and memory copy operations.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Network service
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Denial of Service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Network message containing a payload with a negative length.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static bool
addPayloadToBuffer(CotpConnection* self, uint8_t* buffer,  int payloadLength)
{
    if (payloadLength < 1) {
        if (DEBUG_COTP)
            printf("COTP: missing payload\n");

        return false;
    }

    if (DEBUG_COTP)
        printf("COTP: add to payload buffer (cur size: %i, len: %i)\n", self->payload->size, payloadLength);

    if ((self->payload->size + payloadLength) > self->payload->maxSize)
        return false;

    memcpy(self->payload->buffer + self->payload->size, buffer, payloadLength);

    self->payload->size += payloadLength;

    return true;
}

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Network message
[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write
[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is easy to trigger by sending a specially crafted network packet with a negative payload length, potentially leading to a crash or denial of service.
[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Craft a COTP message where the payload length field is set to a negative integer.
- Transmit the malicious packet to the target service.
- The `addPayloadToBuffer` function receives the negative length.
- The length check `payloadLength < 1` is bypassed if the original vulnerable code is present.
- `memcpy` is called with a large, negative size argument, causing a buffer overflow.
- The service crashes, resulting in a denial of service.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static bool
addPayloadToBuffer(CotpConnection* self, uint8_t* buffer,  int payloadLength)
{
    if (payloadLength < 1) {
        if (DEBUG_COTP)
            printf("COTP: missing payload\n");

        return false;
    }

    if (DEBUG_COTP)
        printf("COTP: add to payload buffer (cur size: %i, len: %i)\n", self->payload->size, payloadLength);

    if ((self->payload->size + payloadLength) > self->payload->maxSize)
        return false;

    memcpy(self->payload->buffer + self->payload->size, buffer, payloadLength);

    self->payload->size += payloadLength;

    return true;
}
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a validation check at the beginning of the function to ensure `payloadLength` is greater than or equal to 1, returning false if it is not. This prevents negative lengths from being processed further.
[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/mz-automation/libiec61850/commit/0b2d97c7383b6aff0057eafcdf2281f717ba0d1a
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- Field: bounds check added

[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the codebase that accept integer length parameters to ensure they validate for non-negative values.
- Fuzz the COTP message parser with payloads containing various integer edge cases (e.g., INT_MIN, -1, 0).
- Diff forks of the repository to check for instances where this fix has not been applied.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar integer validation issues could exist in other network message handling functions.

