The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 Out-of-bounds Read
[Mandatory] Summary:
 The bug is in the `njs_regexp_literal` function in `src/njs_regexp.c`. It is triggered when parsing a regular expression containing a character class (`[...]`) or an escape sequence (`\`). The parser can read past the end of the input buffer because it increments a pointer `p` without first ensuring it has not already reached `lexer->end`. This is exploitable because an attacker can craft a RegExp that causes an out-of-bounds memory read, potentially leading to an information leak or crash.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 The parser increments the character pointer `p` without a sufficient bounds check before dereferencing it.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
 JavaScript engine RegExp parser
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
 Denial of Service (DoS) via potential crash or information leak.
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
 Malformed JavaScript RegExp literal provided to the engine.
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
```c
case '[':
    while (1) {
        if (++p >= lexer->end) {
            goto failed;
        }

        if (*p == ']') {
            break;
        }

        switch (*p) {
        case '\\n':
        case '\\r':
            goto failed;

        case '\\\\':
            if (++p >= lexer->end || *p == '\\n' || *p == '\\r') {
                goto failed;
            }

            break;
        }
    }
```
Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
JavaScript RegExp literal parsing

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds read

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is controlled by the attacker-provided RegExp string and is easy to trigger, but its impact is likely limited to a crash or information leak.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- A crafted RegExp string is created, ending with an unclosed character class `[` or an incomplete escape sequence `\`.
- This string is passed to the njs JavaScript engine for parsing.
- The `njs_regexp_literal` function processes the string.
- When it encounters the `[` or `\`, it enters a loop that increments the pointer `p`.
- The pointer is incremented past the end of the input buffer (`lexer->end`), leading to an out-of-bounds read.
- This results in a segmentation fault or the reading of adjacent memory.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
```c
case '[':
    while (1) {
        if (++p >= lexer->end) {
            goto failed;
        }

        if (*p == ']') {
            break;
        }

        switch (*p) {
        case '\\n':
        case '\\r':
            goto failed;

        case '\\\\':
            if (++p >= lexer->end || *p == '\\n' || *p == '\\r') {
                goto failed;
            }

            break;
        }
    }
```
[Mandatory] Fix summary:
 The patch refactored the loop logic for character classes and escape sequences to explicitly check for the end of the buffer (`p >= lexer->end`) immediately after incrementing the pointer `p`, preventing out-of-bounds reads.

[Optional] Patch reference:
 https://github.com/nginx/njs/commit/ee400ea880dc46bb02c4be2564b183c114d4cd42
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
Bounds check added
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other parsing loops within the `njs` codebase for similar patterns where a pointer is incremented before a bounds check.
- Fuzz the RegExp parser with a focus on edge cases, such as incomplete character classes, escape sequences, and Unicode patterns.
- Diff the fix against other forks or versions of the library to ensure they have incorporated the change.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds reads could exist in other parts of the lexer/parser.

