files.drivers/net/wireless/iwlwifi/iwl-agn-sta:
static int iwl_sta_ucode_activate(struct iwl_priv *priv, u8 sta_id)
{
	if (sta_id >= IWLAGN_STATION_COUNT) {
		IWL_ERR(priv, "invalid sta_id %u", sta_id);
		return -EINVAL;
	}
	if (!(priv->stations[sta_id].used & IWL_STA_DRIVER_ACTIVE))
		IWL_ERR(priv, "ACTIVATE a non DRIVER active station id %u "
			"addr %pM\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);

	if (priv->stations[sta_id].used & IWL_STA_UCODE_ACTIVE) {
		IWL_DEBUG_ASSOC(priv,
				"STA id %u addr %pM already present in uCode "
				"(according to driver)\n",
				sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);
	} else {
		priv->stations[sta_id].used |= IWL_STA_UCODE_ACTIVE;
		IWL_DEBUG_ASSOC(priv, "Added STA id %u addr %pM to uCode\n",
				sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);
	}
	return 0;
}

annotation:
[Mandatory] ID:
CVE-2012-6712

[Mandatory] Title: CVE-2012-6712 / CWE-119:
Memory corruption via invalid sta_id

[Optional] Disclosure / Patch Dates:
Initial advisory: 2019-07-27 — Patch release: NA

[Optional] Affected products/versions:
Linux kernel (torvalds/linux) before commit 2da424b0773cea3db47e1e81db71eeebde8269d4

The Vulnerability
[Optional] CWE/CVE Number and description:
 Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer
[Mandatory] Summary:
 The bug is in the iwl_sta_ucode_activate function in the iwlwifi driver. It is triggered when an invalid sta_id value is passed to the function. The function uses this sta_id as an index into the `priv->stations` array without validating that it is within bounds, leading to an out-of-bounds memory access and potential memory corruption.
[Mandatory] Root cause:
 The function lacks a bounds check on the `sta_id` parameter before using it to index the `priv->stations` array.
[Mandatory] Attack Surface:
Kernel driver (iwlwifi)
[Mandatory] Impact Summary:
Local privilege escalation / Denial of Service
[Optional] Trigger / Attack vector:
Invalid sta_id passed from driver context
[Mandatory] Key code/struct excerpt (minimal):
static int iwl_sta_ucode_activate(struct iwl_priv *priv, u8 sta_id)
{
	if (sta_id >= IWLAGN_STATION_COUNT) {
		IWL_ERR(priv, "invalid sta_id %u", sta_id);
		return -EINVAL;
	}
	if (!(priv->stations[sta_id].used & IWL_STA_DRIVER_ACTIVE))
		IWL_ERR(priv, "ACTIVATE a non DRIVER active station id %u "
			"addr %pM\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);

	if (priv->stations[sta_id].used & IWL_STA_UCODE_ACTIVE) {
		IWL_DEBUG_ASSOC(priv,
			"STA id %u addr %pM already present in uCode "
			"(according to driver)\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);
	} else {
		priv->stations[sta_id].used |= IWL_STA_UCODE_ACTIVE;
		IWL_DEBUG_ASSOC(priv, "Added STA id %u addr %pM to uCode\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);
	}
	return 0;
}

Exploit:

[Mandatory] Trigger surface/attack vector:
Driver function call with a crafted sta_id

[Mandatory] Exploit primitive(s) available:
Out-of-bounds write, information leak

[Mandatory] Exploitability summary (1 sentence):
The exploit is dependent on an attacker's ability to influence the sta_id parameter within the kernel's wireless driver context, which can lead to memory corruption.

[Mandatory] Exploit script (summary, 3—6 bullets):
- Attacker gains ability to call iwlwifi driver functions with controlled parameters.
- A large `sta_id` value (e.g., 148) is supplied to iwl_sta_ucode_activate.
- The function accesses `priv->stations[sta_id]` without a bounds check.
- This results in an out-of-bounds read/write operation on kernel memory.
- Memory corruption can be leveraged for privilege escalation or to crash the system.

Fix
[Mandatory] Fix code:
static int iwl_sta_ucode_activate(struct iwl_priv *priv, u8 sta_id)
{
	if (sta_id >= IWLAGN_STATION_COUNT) {
		IWL_ERR(priv, "invalid sta_id %u", sta_id);
		return -EINVAL;
	}
	if (!(priv->stations[sta_id].used & IWL_STA_DRIVER_ACTIVE))
		IWL_ERR(priv, "ACTIVATE a non DRIVER active station id %u "
			"addr %pM\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);

	if (priv->stations[sta_id].used & IWL_STA_UCODE_ACTIVE) {
		IWL_DEBUG_ASSOC(priv,
			"STA id %u addr %pM already present in uCode "
			"(according to driver)\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);
	} else {
		priv->stations[sta_id].used |= IWL_STA_UCODE_ACTIVE;
		IWL_DEBUG_ASSOC(priv, "Added STA id %u addr %pM to uCode\n",
			sta_id, priv->stations[sta_id].sta.sta.addr);
	}
	return 0;
}

[Mandatory] Fix summary:
The patch adds a bounds check at the beginning of the function to ensure `sta_id` is less than `IWLAGN_STATION_COUNT`. If the check fails, it logs an error and returns -EINVAL, preventing the out-of-bounds access.

[Optional] Patch reference:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/2da424b0773cea3db47e1e81db71eeebde8269d4
[Optional] Patch Analysis:
What did the fix do?
- Field: PatchType (e.g., bounds check added, logic refactor, feature removal)
- Field: bounds check added
[Optional] Variant & Triage Guidance

[Optional] Variant analysis suggestions (2—3 bullets):
- Review other functions in the iwlwifi driver that use `sta_id` as an array index to ensure they have similar bounds checks.
- Fuzz the driver's IOCTL handlers and other entry points to find other ways to trigger an invalid `sta_id`.
- Diff against vendor forks of the Linux kernel to identify if this vulnerability was reintroduced or exists in other versions.

[Optional] Likely variants/impact (one line):
Similar out-of-bounds vulnerabilities may exist in other driver functions that use unvalidated station IDs.

